        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "In many large cities, empty rooftops, balconies and unused <b>plots</b> of land are being turned into small farms. Known as urban farming, this movement began as a hobby among gardening enthusiasts, but it has gradually attracted the attention of city planners, schools and even restaurants. Supporters argue that growing food close to where it is eaten shortens the distance vegetables must travel, which lowers transport costs and the pollution that goes with them.\n\nUrban farming also has a clear educational value. Children who help to plant seeds and harvest crops tend to understand more fully where their food comes from, and they are often more willing to try vegetables they would otherwise refuse. For this reason, a number of schools have built rooftop gardens where lessons in science and nutrition are taught alongside the planting itself. <u>Such hands-on experience cannot easily be replaced by textbooks.</u>\n\nCritics, however, point out that urban farming is not without difficulties. [I] Soil in cities is sometimes contaminated by traffic fumes and old industrial waste, so crops must be tested before they are sold or eaten. [II] Space is also limited, and the amount of food produced on a rooftop is small compared with that of a traditional farm. [III] Water and equipment can be expensive, particularly for beginners who have no experience of growing crops in confined spaces. [IV] These obstacles do not mean that the idea should be abandoned, but they do show that urban farms cannot feed a whole city on their own.\n\nDespite these limitations, urban farms offer something that large-scale agriculture cannot: a visible link between city residents and the food they eat. When people see lettuce growing on the roof of their apartment block, <b>they</b> begin to think about how food is produced and how much of it is wasted. In this sense, the real value of urban farming may lie less in the quantity of food it provides than in the change in attitude it encourages."
