        "passage": "Dr Hoang Mai was born in a small fishing village where the nearest hospital was three hours away by boat. When she was eight, her younger brother died from an illness that could have been treated if help had arrived in time. That loss became the driving force of her life, and from then on she was <b>resolute</b> in her wish to become a doctor and return to serve her community.\n\nHer path was far from easy. Her family could not afford university fees, so she worked as a cleaner in the evenings and shared a small rented room with three other students. More than once she nearly gave up. <u>It was the encouragement of a kind teacher and her own memory of the village that kept her going through those difficult years.</u> She graduated at the top of her class and received several offers from city hospitals.\n\nInstead of accepting any of them, she returned home. [I] With donations from friends and former classmates, she opened a small clinic that treated patients free of charge. [II] At first, many villagers doubted whether a young woman could manage such a place. [III] Her patience and skill gradually won their trust, and soon people from neighbouring villages were arriving at her door. [IV] Today the clinic employs twelve staff and serves more than ten thousand people a year.\n\nDr Mai, however, refuses to be called a hero. She often says that her success belongs to the teachers, donors and volunteers who have supported her, and that <b>they</b> deserve the credit. Her own wish is simple: that every child, in a city or in a remote village, should be able to see a doctor when needed. It is this humble, practical dream that makes her story so inspiring."
