        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "We tend to admire successful people, but we are often more moved by the stories of those who failed repeatedly before they succeeded. A writer whose manuscripts were rejected dozens of times, an athlete who lost many races before winning a title: such stories remind us that success is rarely a straight line.\n\nPsychologists suggest that these stories matter because they change the way we think about failure. People who see failure as a final verdict on their ability often give up quickly, whereas <b>those</b> who see it as a step in learning are more likely to try again. <u>In other words, the same setback can end one person's efforts and motivate another's, depending on how it is understood.</u> This idea is sometimes called a growth mindset.\n\nSharing such stories requires honesty. Many successful people were once <b>reluctant</b> to talk about their mistakes, fearing that others would think less of them. Yet when they finally did, listeners often felt encouraged rather than disappointed. Today, some schools invite former students to speak about the challenges they faced, and teachers report that pupils listen with unusual attention.\n\nOf course, admiring others is useful only if it leads to action. It is easy to feel inspired for an afternoon and then return to old habits. The most lasting inspiration usually turns into <b>tangible</b> steps, such as practising a skill daily, asking for help or starting the project we have been postponing."
