        "passage": "Pham Duc grew up in a coastal village where fishing was the only source of income. As a boy, he spent hours watching the sea, and by the age of ten he could name most of the fish that appeared in his father's nets. When he noticed that catches were becoming smaller every year, he began to ask questions that no one in the village could answer. This <b>curiosity</b> would eventually shape his whole career.\n\nWinning a place at university was far from easy. His school lacked laboratories, and he had to borrow textbooks from a neighbouring town. <u>Rather than complaining about these limits, he taught himself the basics of biology by observing the marine life around him and keeping detailed notes.</u> His notebooks, filled with sketches of shells and records of water temperature, later impressed the professors who interviewed him.\n\n[I] After graduating, Duc returned to his village to study the damage caused by overfishing and warming seas. [II] He found that the coral reefs, which had once sheltered countless fish, were dying. [III] To address the problem, he persuaded fishermen to leave certain areas undisturbed for several months each year. [IV] Within five years, the fish population in those areas had doubled.\n\nHis approach has since been adopted in several other provinces, yet Duc insists that the real credit belongs to the fishermen, and that <b>they</b> deserve the honours he has received. He still lives in his home village, where he trains young people to monitor the health of the reefs. For Duc, the greatest reward is not a prize but the sight of children swimming above reefs that are once again full of life."
