        "passage": "When Thao was fifteen, a <b>severe</b> drought struck her home province, and families walked for hours to collect water that was often dirty. She watched her younger cousins fall ill after drinking it, and she decided to find a solution of her own.\n\nHer first filters, made from sand, charcoal and cloth, were crude and often failed. Teachers politely suggested that she focus on her exams. <u>Instead of abandoning the project, Thao tested each design repeatedly, recording every failure in a notebook and changing only one detail at a time.</u> After two years, she produced a filter that could remove most harmful bacteria at a cost of less than two dollars.\n\n[I] The invention won a national science prize and attracted the attention of newspapers. [II] She spent her university holidays teaching villagers how to make and maintain the filters themselves. [III] Because they could build the devices from local materials, families did not have to depend on outside help. [IV] Within a decade, more than fifty thousand households in dry regions were using them.\n\nThao is now a university lecturer, yet she returns to the villages every year. She checks the filters, listens to families' suggestions and encourages young people to take up science. Asked about her success, she tends to point to the villagers, saying that <b>they</b> proved that an idea becomes valuable only when it is shared."
