        "passage": "Hoa had never seen a piano until the age of thirteen, when a visiting charity left an old electric keyboard at her village school. There was no music teacher, so she taught herself by watching videos on a borrowed phone and copying the finger movements again and again. Her fingers ached and the keyboard was missing several keys, but she <b>persisted</b>.\n\nHer talent was noticed when a visiting musician heard her playing in the school yard. He arranged for her to take lessons in the provincial capital, a journey of four hours each way. <u>Despite the long travel, which meant leaving at dawn and returning after dark, Hoa never missed a single lesson in two years.</u> Her teacher later said that what impressed him most was not her technique but her hunger to learn.\n\n[I] At seventeen, she entered a national competition for young pianists. [II] She was the only contestant who had never studied at a music school, and several judges doubted that she could finish the programme. [III] Yet her performance of a difficult piece moved the audience to tears and won her second prize. [IV] The newspaper that reported the story described her as proof that talent can appear anywhere.\n\nToday, Hoa is a music student in the capital, but she returns to her village every holiday to teach children on the same battered keyboard. She says that <b>they</b> remind her of how much one chance can mean. A local company has recently donated twelve new instruments to the school, and Hoa hopes that one day the village will have its own music room."
