        "passage": "In the mountains of a northern province, children used to see few books apart from their school textbooks. The nearest bookshop was more than eighty kilometres away, and most families could not afford to buy storybooks. When Ms Hanh, a young librarian, arrived to work in the district, she was shocked by this <b>scarcity</b>.\n\nInstead of waiting for the government to build a library, she loaded two boxes of donated books onto her motorbike and began visiting villages one by one. Children gathered around her in yards and under trees, and many of them were reading for pleasure for the first time. <u>Within a year, the requests for new titles had become so numerous that her little motorbike could no longer carry them all.</u>\n\n[I] Her efforts attracted the attention of a publishing company. [II] It agreed to donate five hundred books and a small van. [III] Volunteers from the city came to help her sort the books and to read aloud to the children. [IV] The mobile library now serves twenty-three villages, and each child is allowed to borrow two books at a time.\n\nMs Hanh insists that she is not a hero. She says that the real heroes are the children, who walk long distances to return books on time, and the teachers who encourage <b>them</b> to read at home. Her wish is modest: that every child in the district should have the chance to fall in love with a book."
