        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Every well-known historical achievement rests on the labour of countless people whose names were never recorded. Generals are remembered; the soldiers who carried supplies through impossible conditions rarely are. Inventors receive patents; the technicians who tested and refined their early, failing prototypes are seldom even <b>acknowledged</b>. History, it seems, has always had a narrow definition of who counts as important.\n\nThis pattern is not simply an accident of record-keeping. <u>Those who held positions of visible authority were, almost by definition, more likely to be interviewed, photographed and written about than the people who carried out the practical, unglamorous work beneath them.</u> A general's decisions make for a compelling story; a nurse's thousandth bandage, however essential, rarely does.\n\n[I] In recent decades, however, historians have begun to challenge this narrow focus. [II] Oral history projects now actively seek out the accounts of labourers, caregivers and local organisers who lived through major events. [III] Archives once dedicated almost entirely to famous figures increasingly include letters, diaries and photographs donated by ordinary families. [IV] The resulting picture of the past, while less tidy than a single celebrated narrative, is considerably more complete.\n\nNone of this diminishes the achievements of well-known historical figures. Rather, it situates <b>them</b> within a fuller, more honest picture of how change actually happens: slowly, collectively, and often through the effort of people who never sought credit in the first place. Recognising this does not rewrite history so much as complete it, restoring to the record the countless quiet contributions that made celebrated achievements possible at all."
