        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Few people in the neighbourhood knew much about Mrs Vy before she retired from the local shoe factory at the age of fifty-eight. What began, by her own account, as a way to fill her evenings turned into something far more <b>significant</b>: a free night class for adults who had never finished secondary school.\n\n<u>Word of the class spread slowly at first, mostly among factory workers who had quietly given up on the idea of ever completing their education.</u> Within two years, the small classroom she had set up in a borrowed community room was holding three sessions a week, each one full.\n\nMrs Vy never saw herself as remarkable. She often said that she simply remembered how it felt to leave school early herself, and wanted to spare others the same regret. Former students, however, describe her differently: patient beyond reason, endlessly <b>encouraging</b>, and seemingly incapable of giving up on anyone, however far behind they believed themselves to be.\n\nToday, more than two hundred adults in the neighbourhood credit Mrs Vy's night class with helping them complete their secondary education, several going on to further study of their own. She still teaches twice a week, insisting that <b>she</b> will stop only when nobody shows up anymore. Her story, local teachers often say, is proof that a meaningful life does not require a dramatic stage, only enough persistence to keep showing up for other people."
