        "passage": "When my grandfather passed away, we found a small wooden box beneath his bed containing dozens of letters, none of them ever posted. Reading through them, my family discovered a side of him that decades of ordinary conversation had somehow never <b>revealed</b>: a young man who had once dreamed of becoming a musician before responsibilities quietly redirected his life.\n\n<u>Most of the letters were addressed to a brother who had emigrated overseas shortly after the war, written regularly for nearly a decade despite apparently never being sent.</u> Reading <b>them</b> felt less like discovering historical documents and more like meeting, briefly, a version of my grandfather none of us had ever known.\n\nSome of the letters were practical, describing harvests and family news; others were startlingly personal, admitting doubts and disappointments he had never once mentioned aloud in sixty years of marriage. My grandmother, reading alongside us, seemed as <b>astonished</b> as everyone else, insisting she had never suspected any of it.\n\nWe will probably never know exactly why the letters were never sent, or why he kept writing them anyway. What the box left us, however, was a richer, more complicated portrait of a man we thought we already understood completely. Sometimes, it turns out, the fullest version of someone's story is the one they never got around to telling out loud."
