        "passage": "Scientific history is full of discoveries that were, at the moment of their announcement, dismissed rather than celebrated. A theory later proven correct was, in many such cases, initially regarded as <b>implausible</b>, occasionally even mocked by established experts in the field.\n\nConsider the case of a nineteenth-century doctor who insisted that invisible organisms, far too small to see with the naked eye, were causing a disease then killing large numbers of new mothers in hospitals. <u>Colleagues ridiculed the idea, pointing out that nobody had ever observed such organisms and that the notion seemed closer to superstition than to medicine.</u> Decades later, once microscopes improved and the germ theory of disease became widely accepted, his original insight was finally recognised as correct.\n\n[I] Stories of this kind are not simply historical curiosities. [II] They illustrate a recurring pattern in how scientific communities respond to ideas that challenge existing assumptions. [III] Even solid evidence can struggle to gain acceptance when it conflicts with a theory that the field has already invested heavily in defending. [IV] Only when enough independent evidence accumulates, or when a respected figure finally champions the idea publicly, does resistance typically begin to soften.\n\nNone of this means that scientific scepticism is inherently wrong; healthy doubt protects science from accepting every new claim uncritically. The lesson, rather, is one of <b>humility</b>: today's confidently rejected idea occasionally becomes tomorrow's accepted fact, and the history of science is, in no small part, a history of people who were right before anyone else was willing to admit <b>it</b>."
