        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Few of her university colleagues realised that the quiet, meticulous chemist known for arriving earliest and leaving latest kept two notebooks rather than one. The first, official and carefully organised, recorded the experiments her laboratory was funded to pursue. The second, far messier and entirely unofficial, was where she chased the question that genuinely <b>fascinated</b> her.\n\n<u>For nearly a decade, she pursued her unofficial research quietly during evenings and weekends, convinced that funding committees would never approve a project that seemed, on paper, to have no obvious practical use.</u> Colleagues who glimpsed the second notebook occasionally teased her about her 'hobby,' never suspecting <b>it</b> would eventually matter more than anything in the first.\n\nThe breakthrough, when it finally came, emerged almost by accident, while she was testing an unrelated idea from her official research. Recognising the connection to her private notebook immediately, she spent the following months quietly confirming what she suspected. Her colleagues, reading the resulting paper, were <b>stunned</b> to discover that her 'hobby' had quietly solved a problem the entire field had struggled with for years.\n\nShe later admitted that she might never have made the discovery at all had she waited for official approval before beginning. Her story is now used, somewhat ironically, as an example in grant-writing workshops: proof that the most significant discoveries sometimes begin as the projects nobody was willing to fund."
