        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Children who spend their formative years in a country other than their parents' are often called \"third culture kids\". They may speak one language at home, another at school and a third with their friends, and they usually feel at ease in several cultural settings. This flexibility is a valuable asset in an increasingly <b>interconnected</b> world.\n\nYet the experience is not without difficulty. Because they move frequently, such children may find it hard to say where \"home\" is. <u>Questions as simple as \"Where are you from?\" can feel complicated when the honest answer involves several countries.</u> Some also feel that they do not completely belong to any one culture, and they may experience loneliness when friendships are broken by yet another move.\n\n[I] Researchers have found that these children often develop strong skills in adapting to new situations. [II] They tend to be good listeners, since they have learnt to observe unfamiliar customs before acting. [III] Many also show unusual open-mindedness, accepting differences more readily than their peers. [IV] As adults, they frequently choose careers in diplomacy, international business or education.\n\nSchools and parents can help by acknowledging both the strengths and the struggles of such children. Giving them chances to talk about their experiences, keeping in touch with friends abroad and celebrating the traditions of every place they have lived can help <b>them</b> to build a sense of identity that is not tied to a single address. In this way, home becomes something they carry with them rather than somewhere they leave behind."
