        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Globalisation has made workplaces more diverse than ever before. Large companies employ people from dozens of countries, and even small firms may serve customers or suppliers abroad. Employees may therefore find themselves working with colleagues whose languages, religions and habits differ from their own. For many, this is both an opportunity and a <b>challenge</b>.\n\nThe advantages of diversity are well documented. Teams that include different perspectives tend to notice problems that a more uniform group might miss, and they can design products that appeal to a wider range of customers. <u>Employees who speak several languages or understand foreign markets are especially valuable when a company wants to expand overseas.</u> Working with people from other cultures also broadens one's own outlook.\n\n[I] Yet diversity does not automatically produce success. [II] Differences in communication style can cause confusion: in some cultures, direct disagreement is considered honest, while in others it is thought impolite. [III] Attitudes to time, hierarchy and decision-making also vary. [IV] If managers ignore such differences, tension may grow, and talented employees may feel excluded.\n\nSuccessful organisations therefore invest in intercultural training. Staff learn how different cultures communicate, and managers practise giving feedback in ways that are clear but respectful. Many companies also create mentoring pairs, in which employees from different backgrounds share experience. Employees who feel valued for their differences are more likely to stay and to contribute fully than <b>those</b> who feel they must hide them."
