        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Every culture has its own way of eating, and these habits say a great deal about the people who follow them. The ingredients that are available in a region, its climate and its history all shape the dishes that become traditional. Coastal communities, for example, usually depend on fish, while people in mountainous areas have developed ways of preserving food through long winters. Food therefore tells a story about where people live and how they have <b>adapted</b> to their surroundings.\n\nFood is also closely linked to beliefs and celebrations. In many countries, special dishes are prepared only on particular occasions, and each one carries a meaning. <u>In some places, long noodles are served at birthdays and New Year celebrations as a wish for a long life, while round cakes may stand for the sky or for completeness.</u> Religious rules can also influence what people eat or avoid, and sharing food is often a way of showing hospitality to guests.\n\n[I] Migration has also changed the way people eat. [II] When people settle in a new country, they bring their favourite recipes with them. [III] They therefore replace missing items with local ones, and new versions of old dishes appear. [IV] Over time, some of these dishes become so popular that people forget their foreign origins.\n\nToday, restaurants, television programmes and websites allow people to taste dishes from all over the world. Trying unfamiliar food can make us more open-minded, because it encourages us to respect the habits of other people. However, some experts warn that family recipes may be lost if young people choose fast food rather than learn to cook the dishes their grandparents once prepared. Sharing meals is therefore important: families who cook together keep traditions alive, and <b>they</b> also strengthen their relationships."
