        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Language is not only a tool for communication; it also carries the values and history of the people who speak it. Many words reflect what a community considers important. Vietnamese, for instance, has a rich system of pronouns that show a speaker's age and relationship to other people, which <b>reveals</b> how much respect is valued within the family and in society.\n\nIdioms offer another window into culture. They often cannot be understood by translating each word separately, because their meanings come from shared experience. <u>An English speaker who says that something is 'a piece of cake' is talking about an easy task, not about food, and a learner who does not know this may feel confused.</u> For this reason, language teachers encourage students to learn expressions in context rather than as separate words.\n\n[I] Politeness is also expressed differently across languages. [II] In some languages, speakers choose different words depending on whether they are talking to a friend or to an older person. [III] Learners who ignore these habits may sound rude without intending to. [IV] Teachers therefore advise students to observe how native speakers address people in different situations.\n\nLearning a language therefore means more than memorising vocabulary and grammar. It means entering a different way of seeing the world. Students who study culture along with language tend to communicate more effectively, and <b>they</b> are less likely to cause misunderstandings. Many schools now organise exchange programmes for this reason, because living in another country is one of the quickest ways to understand how language and culture are connected."
