        "passage": "English has become the main language of international communication. It is <b>widely</b> used in science, business, aviation and on the internet, and millions of people who do not speak it as their first language learn it in order to study or work abroad. This situation has made it easier for people from different countries to share ideas.\n\nHowever, the spread of English has a price. In many places, children are encouraged to study in English from an early age, and they sometimes use their mother tongue less and less. <u>When young people stop speaking the language of their grandparents, the stories, songs and traditions expressed in that language may be lost for ever.</u> Linguists warn that many of the world's languages may disappear within a few generations.\n\nSome countries have taken action to protect their languages. They teach minority languages in primary schools, broadcast programmes in them and support writers who publish books in these languages. Other communities use technology: they record elderly speakers, build online dictionaries and create mobile applications that make learning <b>enjoyable</b> for the young.\n\nExperts agree that a person can value both English and a native language. Speaking several languages is an advantage: bilingual people often find it easier to learn new languages and to understand other cultures. Parents therefore play an important role. If <b>they</b> speak their mother tongue at home, children grow up with a strong sense of identity and can pass it on to the next generation."
