        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Traditional music carries the memories of a community. Songs that were once sung in fields, at weddings or during festivals tell us how people lived, worked and celebrated. In Viet Nam, for example, folk styles such as quan ho, which comes from the northern province of Bac Ninh, are performed by men and women who sing in turn, answering each other's songs. UNESCO has <b>recognised</b> quan ho as part of the world's intangible cultural heritage.\n\nToday, however, many young people prefer pop and hip-hop to traditional songs. Some musicians have responded by blending the two. They add electronic beats to folk melodies or invite traditional instrumentalists to play in modern bands. <u>Such experiments can attract young listeners who might otherwise never have paid attention to the music of their ancestors.</u> At the same time, they give older styles a chance to be heard around the world.\n\n[I] Some people worry that such blending damages tradition. [II] They fear that the original meaning of a song may be lost when it is changed too much. [III] Musicians who work in both worlds often study the old styles carefully before they begin to experiment. [IV] In this way, they show respect for tradition while still creating something new.\n\nThe debate will probably continue, but one fact is clear: music keeps changing because the people who make it keep changing. Schools can help by teaching children both traditional songs and modern styles. Children who learn to value different kinds of music grow up open-minded, and <b>they</b> are more likely to pass on the songs they have learned."
