        "passage": "Viet Nam is home to fifty-four officially recognised ethnic groups. The Kinh people are the largest group and live mainly in the deltas and cities, while the other fifty-three groups are spread across mountainous areas, highlands and some coastal regions. Each group has its own language, costumes and customs, and together they form a <b>diverse</b> cultural landscape.\n\nThe customs of these groups are rich and varied. The Tay and Thai peoples, for example, usually live in stilt houses, while communities in the Central Highlands have traditionally built tall communal houses at the centre of the village. Gongs, which are played at important ceremonies, have been recognised by UNESCO as part of the world's cultural heritage. <u>Despite their differences, the groups have lived side by side for centuries, exchanging products, ideas and traditions.</u>\n\n[I] Modern life is bringing many changes to ethnic communities. [II] Roads and electricity have improved access to schools and hospitals. [III] As a result, some traditional costumes are now worn only on festival days. [IV] Community leaders therefore worry that customs may slowly be forgotten.\n\nFortunately, many communities are finding ways to keep their cultures alive. Local schools teach children traditional songs and dances, and cultural centres organise festivals that attract visitors from across the country. Tourism also provides an income, so that families can earn a living without leaving their villages. Villagers who can earn money from their traditions have a good reason to preserve <b>them</b>."
