        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "More companies now do business with partners in other countries, so understanding foreign business customs has become a valuable skill. Even small details can influence the success of a meeting. In Japan, for example, business cards are exchanged with both hands and read carefully before being put away, because the card is seen as a representation of the person. Treating it <b>casually</b> may suggest a lack of respect.\n\nAttitudes to time also differ. In some countries, arriving a few minutes early shows seriousness, while in others a meeting is expected to begin late and relationships matter more than schedules. <u>Visitors who judge their hosts by their own standards of punctuality may draw wrong conclusions about their reliability and interest.</u> It is therefore wiser to ask about local expectations in advance.\n\n[I] Gift-giving is another area in which customs vary. [II] In some countries, a gift is opened only after the guests have left. [III] Certain colours or numbers may also have unlucky meanings, so it is wise to check before buying a present. [IV] A small, thoughtful gift is usually appreciated everywhere, provided that it is chosen with care.\n\nLearning these customs is not about memorising lists of rules. It is about showing that you are willing to respect the people you work with. Businesspeople who make this effort tend to build stronger relationships, and <b>they</b> are more likely to succeed in the long term."
