        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Tourism brings money and jobs to many communities, but it can also cause problems. Large numbers of visitors may damage historic sites, create rubbish and make local life more expensive. In some popular destinations, residents complain that they can no longer enjoy the places where they live. Responsible tourism aims to <b>reduce</b> these harmful effects while keeping the benefits.\n\nResponsible travellers begin by choosing local services. Staying in family-run guesthouses, eating in neighbourhood restaurants and buying souvenirs made by local craftspeople ensures that more of the money stays in the community. <u>Travellers should also respect local rules, such as dressing modestly at temples and asking permission before taking photographs of people.</u> Such small actions show that visitors value the culture they have come to see.\n\nProtecting the environment is equally important. Visitors should avoid leaving rubbish, keep to marked paths and refuse to buy products made from endangered animals or plants. In some national parks, the number of daily visitors is <b>limited</b> so that nature has time to recover.\n\nTour operators and governments also have responsibilities. They can educate visitors, train local guides and share profits fairly. When tourists, businesses and residents work together, <b>they</b> can create a form of travel that benefits everyone."
