        "passage": "Every year, millions of students leave their home countries to study abroad. They are attracted by the quality of foreign universities, the chance to improve their language skills and the hope of a better career. For many, however, the first months are not easy. Everything from the food to the way lessons are taught may seem <b>unfamiliar</b>, and understanding lectures in a second language can be tiring.\n\nHomesickness is another common problem. Students may miss their families, their friends and the comfort of familiar surroundings. <u>Those who stay in close contact with home but also make an effort to join local activities usually adjust more quickly than those who isolate themselves.</u> Universities often help by organising orientation weeks, buddy schemes and clubs for international students.\n\n[I] Despite these difficulties, studying abroad offers great rewards. [II] Students gain independence because they must manage their own money, time and daily lives. [III] Students also develop a more global outlook through friendships with people from many countries. [IV] These advantages explain why so many young people still choose to study overseas.\n\nUniversities and governments are keen to attract international students, but welcoming them involves more than offering places. Students need clear information, affordable housing and support when problems arise. Host communities, too, can make a difference: friendly neighbours who invite students to local celebrations help <b>them</b> to feel at home."
