        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Many schools today welcome students from several countries, so teachers are trying to make lessons reflect this <b>diversity</b>. Multicultural education means more than teaching about festivals and food. It involves using examples, stories and role models from many cultures, so that every child can see his or her background in the classroom.\n\nTeachers' experience suggests that this approach has clear benefits. Students who feel that their culture is respected tend to take a more active part in lessons. <u>Classmates who learn about each other's traditions are less likely to hold stereotypes and more likely to build friendships across cultural lines.</u> Teachers also report fewer conflicts in classes where differences are discussed openly.\n\nPutting these ideas into practice is not always easy. Teachers may have had little training in how to <b>handle</b> cultural differences, and textbooks often present only one point of view. Some parents worry that time spent on other cultures will reduce the time available for traditional subjects.\n\nDespite these challenges, many schools have found simple solutions. They invite parents to share their traditions, set up language clubs and choose reading lists that include authors from several continents. When families feel welcome, <b>they</b> become partners in education rather than outsiders."
