        "passage": "Weddings are celebrated in every culture, but the customs surrounding them differ widely. In India, brides often decorate their hands and feet with henna patterns, and the celebrations may last several days. In many Western countries, the bride traditionally wears a white dress, while in Viet Nam she often wears a red or pink ao dai for part of the ceremony. Whatever the <b>style</b>, these customs express hopes for a happy and lasting marriage.\n\nMany customs carry symbolic meanings. In Viet Nam, families exchange gifts such as tea, fruit and betel leaves during an engagement ceremony, and each item has a special meaning. <u>Such rituals show that marriage is seen not only as a union between two people but also as a bond between two families.</u> Similar ideas can be found in many other cultures.\n\n[I] Modern couples often combine old and new traditions. [II] Some hold a traditional ceremony at home and then a party in a hotel with friends and colleagues. [III] Others invite guests from abroad and include customs from the cultures of both families. [IV] These mixed ceremonies show how traditions can adapt without being lost.\n\nWedding customs also reflect changes in society. Not long ago, the choice of a partner was often made by parents, but today most young people choose for <b>themselves</b>. Yet respect for family remains central in many countries, and couples usually ask for their parents' blessing before they marry."
