        "passage": "Social media has made it easier than ever to learn about other cultures. A student in Viet Nam can follow a chef in Mexico, watch a dance performance from Korea or join an online discussion with classmates in Canada. Such contact was once <b>impossible</b> without travelling abroad.\n\nThese platforms also help communities to share their own traditions with a wider audience. Craftspeople post videos of their work, musicians upload recordings of folk songs and local cooks explain family recipes. <u>Many of them have gained new customers and admirers simply because people around the world were able to watch what they do.</u> In this way, small cultures can become visible.\n\nNevertheless, online contact has its limits. Short videos may show only the most colourful side of a culture and ignore its complexity. Users sometimes jump to conclusions or repeat stereotypes without checking whether they are <b>accurate</b>. Some also receive hostile comments when they share cultural content that others do not understand.\n\nTo benefit fully, users should treat social media as a starting point rather than as the final word. They can verify information from reliable sources, ask polite questions and listen to people who belong to the culture concerned. Combined with real travel and personal friendships, online exchange can help <b>them</b> to develop a deeper understanding of the world."
