        "passage": "Clothes are cheaper and more plentiful than ever. Fast-fashion companies produce new collections every few weeks, tempting shoppers to buy more than they need. The result is a mountain of discarded clothing: millions of tonnes of textiles are thrown away every year, and much of it ends up in landfill, where synthetic fibres can take generations to <b>decompose</b>.\n\nThe environmental costs begin long before clothes reach the shop. Growing cotton requires large amounts of water, and dyeing fabrics can pollute rivers if waste is not treated properly. Transporting garments across the world adds further emissions. <u>Cheap prices therefore hide the true cost of a T-shirt, which is paid by the environment and, often, by underpaid workers.</u>\n\n[I] A growing number of consumers are looking for alternatives. [II] Second-hand shops and online resale platforms give clothes a second life at a fraction of the price. [III] Clothing swaps allow friends to exchange items they no longer wear. [IV] Some brands now offer repair services, encouraging customers to mend rather than replace.\n\nUltimately, the most sustainable garment is the one that already exists in a wardrobe. Buying fewer, better-made items and wearing them for longer reduces waste more effectively than recycling can. As more shoppers adopt this attitude, <b>they</b> send a clear message to manufacturers that quality matters more than quantity."
