        "passage": "In recent years, a growing number of people have chosen to eat less meat. Some give it up completely, while others follow the principle of \"flexitarianism\", eating mainly vegetables but enjoying meat occasionally. Their reasons vary: health, animal welfare and, increasingly, concern for the environment.\n\nLivestock farming uses large areas of land and water and produces significant quantities of greenhouse gases. Scientists estimate that producing a kilogram of beef requires far more resources than producing a kilogram of beans or lentils. <u>Reducing meat consumption, even slightly, could therefore lower the pressure that food production puts on the planet.</u> Environmental groups welcome this trend, but <b>they</b> admit that changing eating habits is difficult.\n\nHowever, a plant-based diet is not automatically green. Vegetables flown in from another continent or grown in heated greenhouses may use more energy than local produce. <b>Excessive</b> packaging and food waste can also reduce the benefits. Supporters of local food argue that buying seasonal products from nearby farms is often the more sustainable choice.\n\nIndividuals do not have to choose between extremes. Setting aside a meat-free day each week, favouring seasonal products and wasting less food are all realistic steps. Such <b>modest</b> changes may seem insignificant, but when millions of people make them together, the effect can be considerable."
