        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "The zero-waste movement began as a challenge: could a household live for a year while producing only a small jar of rubbish? Followers answer by rethinking every purchase. They buy food in bulk with their own containers, choose products without packaging, and replace disposable items with durable ones such as cloth bags, metal straws and refillable bottles. The aim is not <b>perfection</b> but a steady reduction in waste.\n\nWaste is more than an eyesore. Producing, transporting and disposing of goods consumes energy and raw materials, and rubbish in landfills releases methane, a greenhouse gas. <u>By refusing unnecessary items in the first place, zero-waste households tackle the problem at its source instead of dealing with its consequences afterwards.</u> They also save money, since they buy less and reuse more.\n\n[I] Beginning does not require a dramatic change. [II] Many people start by carrying a reusable bag and bottle and by refusing plastic straws. [III] Later steps may include composting kitchen waste and repairing clothes. [IV] Each success builds confidence for the next.\n\nCritics point out that individual choices cannot solve the problem alone, since most waste is produced by industry and construction. They also note that some zero-waste products are expensive and are not available to everyone. Supporters reply that personal action and pressure on companies and governments go hand in hand. When consumers refuse excessive packaging, <b>they</b> send a message that producers cannot ignore."
