        "passage": "Buildings account for a large share of energy use and greenhouse gas emissions worldwide. Lighting, cooling, heating and the production of materials such as cement and steel all contribute. As a result, architects and homeowners are paying more attention to how houses can be designed to use less energy and fewer resources.\n\nA green home begins with good design. Large windows bring in daylight, reducing the need for electric lighting, and thick walls and shaded roofs keep rooms cool without constant air conditioning. In hot countries, traditional houses that were built with natural ventilation often perform better than <b>those</b> that depend entirely on machines. <u>Using local materials such as bamboo, wood and clay can also reduce emissions, because they require less energy to produce and transport.</u>\n\nTechnology can add further savings. Solar panels on the roof produce electricity, and efficient appliances use much less power than older models. Smart meters show families how much energy they are using, which often <b>encourages</b> them to waste less. Rainwater tanks and water-saving taps reduce demand for the public water supply.\n\nOf course, green features can increase the cost of building. However, the extra expense is usually recovered through lower bills, and healthier indoor air and greater comfort are valuable <b>bonuses</b>. Governments can speed up the change by offering tax benefits and by setting higher standards for new buildings, so that sustainable design becomes normal rather than exceptional."
