        "passage": "Fossil fuels such as coal, oil and gas have powered industry for more than two centuries, but burning them releases greenhouse gases that warm the planet. As a result, many countries are turning to renewable sources of energy, which come from natural processes that are constantly <b>replenished</b>. These include sunlight, wind and flowing water.\n\nSolar panels convert sunlight directly into electricity, and they can be installed on the roofs of houses, schools and factories. Wind turbines, meanwhile, use the movement of air to turn generators, and they work best on coasts and hills. <u>In recent years, the costs of both technologies have fallen so quickly that they now compete with fossil fuels in many parts of the world.</u> Viet Nam has also seen a rapid increase in solar and wind power.\n\n[I] Renewable energy is not without problems. [II] The sun does not shine at night, and the wind does not always blow. [III] Building solar and wind farms also requires a great deal of land and money at the beginning. [IV] Nevertheless, most experts believe that these difficulties can be overcome through better technology.\n\nGovernments can encourage the change by offering financial support to families that install solar panels. Schools can teach young people about the benefits of clean energy, and companies can invest in research. When more people understand the issue, <b>they</b> are more likely to support policies that protect the environment."
