        "passage": "Transport is responsible for a large share of the greenhouse gases that cause climate change. Cars, lorries and planes burn petrol, diesel or aviation fuel, and in crowded cities the exhaust fumes also harm people's health. Finding cleaner ways to move people and goods has therefore become a <b>priority</b> for governments around the world.\n\nOne solution is public transport. Trains, metros and buses carry many passengers at once, so they use less fuel per person than private cars. <u>Cities that invest in reliable and affordable public transport often find that fewer residents feel the need to own a car.</u> Cycling and walking, which produce no emissions at all, are also encouraged through bicycle lanes and wider pavements.\n\n[I] Electric vehicles offer another solution. [II] They run on batteries and release no exhaust gases while driving. [III] If this electricity comes from coal, much of the pollution is simply moved from the road to the power station. [IV] Governments therefore need to expand renewable power at the same time as they promote electric cars.\n\nIndividuals can also play a part. Choosing a bicycle for a short trip, sharing a car with colleagues or taking the bus once or twice a week all help to reduce emissions. Although such choices may seem small, <b>they</b> become significant when millions of people make them."
