        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Every year, a surprisingly large amount of the food that people produce is never eaten. Some is lost on farms and during transport, but a great deal is thrown away in homes, shops and restaurants. This waste is a problem for several reasons. First, the land, water and energy used to produce the food are wasted as well. Second, food that rots in landfills releases methane, a gas that <b>contributes</b> to global warming.\n\nIn many households, waste begins in the shop. People buy more than they need because of special offers or because they plan meals badly. <u>Misunderstanding the dates printed on packages is another cause, since many products are still safe to eat after the 'best before' date has passed.</u> As a result, perfectly good food ends up in the bin.\n\nFortunately, there are practical solutions. Making a shopping list, storing food properly and using leftovers in new dishes can greatly reduce waste at home. Restaurants can offer smaller portions, and supermarkets can sell damaged but edible fruit and vegetables at lower prices. Some organisations collect surplus food and <b>distribute</b> it to people in need.\n\nComposting is another useful method. Vegetable peels and other organic waste can be turned into rich soil for gardens instead of being sent to landfills. Schools that start composting programmes often find that students become more aware of the problem, and <b>they</b> take the lessons home to their families."
