        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "In many crowded cities, open land is scarce, yet more and more residents are growing their own vegetables. They use rooftops, balconies, backyards and even walls. This trend, known as urban gardening, is <b>spreading</b> rapidly because it offers benefits to both people and the environment.\n\nUrban gardens produce fresh food close to where people live, so it does not have to be transported over long distances. This reduces the fuel used for delivery and the packaging needed to keep the food fresh. <u>Plants also absorb carbon dioxide, cool the air around them and provide a habitat for insects and birds that might otherwise find little to live on in a city.</u> In addition, gardening is a relaxing hobby that helps to reduce stress.\n\nVertical farming takes the idea a step further. Plants are grown in stacked layers inside buildings, often without soil. LED lights replace sunlight, and a closed water system recycles most of the water used, so these farms need far less water than traditional fields. However, the lights and equipment require a lot of electricity, which makes the system <b>costly</b> unless the energy comes from renewable sources.\n\nCommunity gardens are also valuable socially. Neighbours who share a plot meet regularly, exchange tips and sometimes share their harvest. Elderly residents can pass on their knowledge to young people, and <b>they</b> in turn bring energy and new ideas."
