        "passage": "The Mekong Delta, often called the rice bowl of Viet Nam, is one of the most productive farming regions in Southeast Asia. Its low, flat land is crossed by thousands of canals and rivers, which makes it ideal for growing rice and raising fish. However, the same geography makes the delta extremely <b>vulnerable</b> to rising sea levels.\n\nAs the sea rises, salt water moves further inland during the dry season. This process, called salinity intrusion, damages crops and spoils the fresh water that farmers and families rely on. <u>In some areas, farmers whose rice fields have become too salty have switched to shrimp farming or to crops that can tolerate salt.</u> These changes help in the short term, but they require new skills and investment.\n\n[I] Solutions are being developed at several levels. [II] Engineers are building sluice gates and dykes to keep salt water out of the fields. [III] Planting mangrove forests along the coast offers a cheaper and more natural form of protection. [IV] Their roots hold the soil in place and reduce the force of the waves.\n\nIndividual farmers, too, are adapting. Many now follow weather forecasts closely, store fresh water in ponds and plant rice varieties that mature faster. When communities share information about <b>their</b> experiences, others can learn from successful methods and avoid costly mistakes."
