        "passage": "Ecotourism is a form of travel that aims to protect nature and support local communities. Unlike mass tourism, which may <b>damage</b> fragile environments, ecotourism limits the number of visitors and uses the income to protect wildlife and habitats. Travellers usually stay in small lodges built with local materials and learn about the area from local guides.\n\nFor local communities, ecotourism can be a valuable source of income. Residents work as guides, cooks and boat drivers, and they sell handicrafts to visitors. <u>When people can earn a living from healthy forests or clean beaches, they have less reason to cut trees or catch endangered animals illegally.</u> In this way, conservation and development support each other.\n\nNot every project, however, deserves the name. Some companies use the word 'eco' only as a marketing tool, while their hotels waste water and energy. Visitors can avoid such 'greenwashing' by asking how a business protects the environment, whether it employs local people and what happens to its waste. Operators with a <b>genuine</b> commitment to ecotourism are usually happy to answer such questions.\n\nTourists themselves can make a difference. They should follow park rules, keep a safe distance from animals and take their rubbish with them. When visitors behave responsibly, <b>they</b> set an example that others may follow, and local people see that their environment is valued."
