        "passage": "Hydropower is the world's largest source of renewable electricity. It works by using the force of falling or flowing water to turn turbines, which in turn power generators. Because rain and snow keep filling rivers, the supply of water is <b>sustainable</b> as long as the climate remains stable.\n\nViet Nam has made extensive use of its rivers, and hydropower stations provide a large share of the country's electricity. They also help to control floods and store water for farming. <u>Large dams, however, can force thousands of people to leave their homes and may change the flow of rivers in ways that harm fish and farmland downstream.</u> For these reasons, planners now try to weigh the benefits against the costs more carefully.\n\n[I] Wind power has a different set of advantages and drawbacks. [II] Wind turbines need no fuel and produce no pollution while they operate. [III] Some people also complain that turbines are noisy or spoil the view. [IV] Better designs and careful planning can reduce these concerns.\n\nNeither source can meet a country's needs alone. Wind and water can complement each other, because the weather conditions that reduce one source may not affect the other. Combining several renewable sources therefore gives a more reliable supply. Many governments are planning a mix of different technologies, and <b>they</b> expect it to lower costs as well."
