        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Fresh water is essential for life, but only a small fraction of the water on Earth is suitable for drinking and farming. In many regions, growing populations, pollution and changing rainfall are increasing the <b>pressure</b> on this limited resource. Saving water has therefore become a matter of urgent concern.\n\nHouseholds can save a great deal of water through simple habits. Turning off the tap while brushing teeth, taking shorter showers and repairing leaking pipes quickly all reduce waste. <u>A dripping tap may seem harmless, but over a year it can waste enough water to fill many bathtubs.</u> Washing full loads of laundry and collecting rainwater for the garden are other easy steps.\n\nAgriculture uses a larger share of fresh water than any other activity, so farmers have an important role. Drip irrigation, which delivers water directly to the roots of plants, uses far less water than flooding entire fields. Growing crops that are suited to local conditions also helps. Some regions have started to <b>recycle</b> treated wastewater for irrigation, which reduces the demand on rivers and wells.\n\nGovernments and businesses must also act. They can fix leaks in city pipe systems, charge fair prices for water so that it is not wasted and invest in clean technology. When consumers see how much water a product requires to make, <b>they</b> may choose more carefully."
