        "passage": "The Can Gio mangrove forest, south-east of Ho Chi Minh City, is often described as a conservation success story. Large areas were destroyed by chemicals during the war, and in the years that followed some of the remaining trees were cut for fuel. Beginning in the late 1970s, however, local people and the government began to replant the forest, and within a few decades it had <b>recovered</b> so well that UNESCO recognised it as a biosphere reserve in 2000.\n\nToday the forest plays several roles. Its dense roots slow down waves during storms, protecting villages and farmland behind it. It filters water and traps sediment, which helps to keep the nearby coast stable. <u>The forest also provides a breeding ground for fish, crabs and shrimp, and these in turn support the livelihoods of local fishermen.</u> Many species of birds and reptiles also live there.\n\n[I] Tourism has brought new opportunities to the area. [II] Visitors can take boat trips along the channels and observe monkeys, birds and crabs. [III] To limit this damage, the reserve has set rules about where boats may go and how many people may visit at one time. [IV] Income from tickets is used to pay for protection and for the work of local guides.\n\nThe story of Can Gio shows that damaged ecosystems can recover if people are patient and committed. It also shows that conservation works best when it benefits local communities. Villagers who earn a living from the forest through fishing, tourism or beekeeping have good reasons to protect <b>it</b>."
