        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Ordinary plastic is made from oil, and it can take hundreds of years to break down. In response, scientists have developed bioplastics, which are made partly or entirely from renewable plants such as corn, sugarcane and cassava. Because plants absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, producing plastic from them can <b>lower</b> the amount of fossil fuel that is needed.\n\nHowever, not all bioplastics behave in the same way. Some are biodegradable, which means that microorganisms can break them down into natural substances. <u>Others look and feel like normal plastic and last just as long, so calling them 'green' can be misleading.</u> Many biodegradable types also need the high temperatures of industrial composting plants and will not break down quickly in a garden, in a landfill or in the sea.\n\nAnother concern is land use. Growing crops for plastic requires fields, water and fertiliser, and these may compete with food production. Researchers are therefore looking for alternatives, such as using agricultural waste, seaweed or even the shells of crabs and shrimps. These materials do not require extra farmland and may be <b>abundant</b> in places where seafood is processed.\n\nConsumers can help by reading labels carefully and by disposing of packaging correctly. Even the best material cannot solve the problem if people throw it away carelessly. Experts agree that reducing and reusing remain better choices than relying on new materials, and <b>they</b> advise people to avoid unnecessary packaging in the first place."
