        "passage": "Rivers provide drinking water, food and transport for millions of people, yet many of them are seriously polluted. Waste from factories often contains chemicals and heavy metals, while farms may release fertilisers and pesticides that wash into streams after rain. In addition, households in many places still <b>discharge</b> untreated wastewater and rubbish directly into rivers and canals.\n\nThe effects are serious. Polluted water kills fish and other aquatic life, and people who drink it or use it for washing may suffer from stomach diseases and skin problems. <u>Fertilisers can also cause algae to grow so fast that they use up the oxygen in the water, leaving little for fish to breathe.</u> The cost of cleaning contaminated water for drinking is high, and poorer communities often cannot afford it.\n\n[I] Many countries have introduced stricter rules to protect their rivers. [II] Factories must now treat their wastewater before releasing it, and those who break the rules may be fined. [III] Technology can help, for example by using sensors that measure water quality continuously. [IV] Such systems warn authorities immediately when pollution levels suddenly rise.\n\nIndividuals can also protect rivers. Using fewer chemical cleaners, never dumping oil or rubbish into drains and joining river clean-up days all make a difference. Communities that take pride in their rivers often organise regular clean-ups, and <b>they</b> find that water quality improves when people feel responsible for it."
