        "passage": "In the 1980s, scientists discovered a large hole in the ozone layer above Antarctica. This layer of gas, high in the atmosphere, protects living things from the sun's harmful ultraviolet rays. The cause was a group of man-made chemicals, known as CFCs, which were widely used in refrigerators, air conditioners and spray cans. The discovery caused <b>alarm</b>, because more ultraviolet radiation can lead to skin cancer and damage crops and sea life.\n\nThe world's response was remarkably quick. In 1987, countries signed an international agreement, the Montreal Protocol, to stop producing CFCs. <u>Industries developed safer alternatives, and governments checked that the new rules were followed, so the amount of harmful chemicals in the atmosphere gradually began to fall.</u> Today, almost every country in the world has joined the agreement.\n\n[I] The results have been encouraging. [II] Measurements show that the hole above Antarctica has begun to shrink. [III] The agreement is also considered a success because it brought rival countries together. [IV] It has therefore become a model for other international efforts, such as the fight against climate change.\n\nThe story of the ozone layer shows that global problems can be solved when governments, scientists and industries work together. It also shows the importance of acting on scientific warnings early. Many experts hope that the same approach can be applied to climate change, although <b>they</b> admit that the problem is far more complex."
