        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Every year, millions of people around the world leave villages for cities. Geographers explain this movement through push and pull factors. Push factors are the difficulties that encourage people to leave rural areas, such as low incomes, <b>limited</b> land and a lack of schools and hospitals. Pull factors are the attractions of urban life, including a wider choice of jobs, better services and the hope of a higher standard of living.\n\nFor many migrants, the move brings real benefits. Factory workers send money home, helping their families to build better houses and pay for their children's education. Cities also provide access to hospitals, universities and cultural activities that rural areas rarely offer. <u>Without this steady flow of young workers, many industries in urban areas would struggle to find the labour they need.</u>\n\nHowever, migration also creates difficulties for both the places that people leave and those that they enter. [I] Villages lose their youngest and most energetic residents, so that farms are left to older people. [II] Meanwhile, cities must provide housing, transport and jobs for newcomers, and <b>they</b> often fail to keep pace. [III] Some migrants therefore find only crowded neighbourhoods with poor sanitation and unstable work. [IV] Their children may then grow up without access to adequate schools or clinics.\n\nGovernments therefore try to manage migration instead of simply trying to stop it. Investing in rural roads, schools and clinics can make villages more attractive, while building affordable housing and public transport in cities helps newcomers to settle. Creating new industrial and service centres in smaller towns can also reduce pressure on the largest cities. In this way, the benefits of urban growth can be shared more evenly."
