        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "A city is more than a collection of buildings and roads. It is a place where people meet, work, relax and form communities. Planners increasingly recognise that public spaces such as squares, parks and pavements are as important to a city's quality of life as housing and transport.\n\nWell-designed public spaces bring many benefits. They give residents a place to exercise, talk and enjoy fresh air, which is especially valuable in crowded districts where homes are small. People who live near parks tend to be more physically active than <b>those</b> who live far from them. <u>When public spaces are safe and attractive, they also encourage neighbours to meet one another, which strengthens trust and a sense of belonging.</u>\n\nUnfortunately, public space is often the first thing to disappear when land becomes expensive. Developers may prefer to build shops or apartments on every available plot, and in some neighbourhoods, pavements are occupied by parked vehicles or street stalls. Pedestrians are then forced to walk on the road, which is both <b>hazardous</b> and unpleasant. Without protection, the shared areas of a city can be gradually lost.\n\nTo prevent this, many cities now set minimum standards for green space and protect important squares by law. Others invite residents to help decide how local spaces should be used, for example by turning an empty lot into a playground or a market. Projects of this kind are most successful when they respond to what people actually <b>require</b>, not what planners assume they want."
