        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Traffic jams are a daily experience for millions of city dwellers. In the busiest cities, commuters may spend more than an hour each way in slow-moving traffic, wasting time, fuel and patience. Congestion also raises the cost of doing business, since delivery vehicles and workers arrive late.\n\nThe causes are complex. Cities have grown faster than roads, and many residents prefer private vehicles because public transport is crowded or unreliable. Motorbikes and cars occupy much more road space per passenger than buses do, so a street full of private vehicles carries fewer people than <b>those</b> served by buses. <u>Building more roads rarely solves the problem for long, because new roads attract more vehicles and soon become as crowded as the old ones.</u>\n\nMore effective measures focus on moving people rather than vehicles. Metro lines, bus lanes and bicycle paths give commuters real alternatives. Some cities charge drivers a fee for entering the centre at peak hours, while others limit the number of vehicles allowed on the road each day. Employers can <b>mitigate</b> the problem by allowing flexible hours and remote work, so that fewer people travel at the same time.\n\nSuch policies are rarely popular at first, because they change habits and may seem unfair to drivers. Successful cities therefore improve alternatives before introducing restrictions and explain the reasons clearly to the public. Over time, many residents discover that a reliable bus or metro is not only cheaper but also more <b>predictable</b> than driving through traffic."
