        "passage": "A smart city uses digital technology to make urban services more efficient and to improve the quality of life of its residents. Thousands of sensors, cameras and connected devices collect information about traffic, energy use, air quality and waste. This information is then analysed by computers, which help city managers to make decisions more quickly. Cities such as Singapore, Seoul and Barcelona are often <b>cited</b> as examples of how this approach can work.\n\nSmart technology can save both time and money. Smart traffic lights, for example, adjust to the number of vehicles at a crossing, so drivers wait for less time. Street lamps that dim when no one is nearby use less electricity, and sensors in rubbish bins tell collectors when they are full. <u>Instead of following a fixed timetable, workers can go only where they are needed, which saves fuel and labour.</u> Residents benefit too, because they can use apps to find parking spaces, pay bills and report problems.\n\n[I] Building a smart city is not easy, however. [II] The technology is expensive, and many cities in developing countries cannot afford to install it everywhere. [III] There is also the question of personal data, since cameras and apps can record where people go and what they do. [IV] Many residents are therefore worried about who can see this information and how it is used.\n\nDespite these problems, many experts believe that smart technology will become more common. Some cities start with small projects, such as smart street lighting, and expand them step by step. Others invite residents to give their opinions before any new system is introduced, since <b>they</b> are more willing to accept changes that they have helped to plan."
