        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "As cities grow, so does the amount of pollution they produce. Vehicles, factories and construction sites release gases and fine dust into the air, and the large amount of electricity used in homes and offices adds to the problem. In some of the world's biggest cities, pollution is so <b>severe</b> that people are advised to stay indoors on the worst days.\n\nPolluted air harms health in many ways. It can cause coughing and breathing difficulties, and over many years it may contribute to heart disease and lung cancer. <u>Children and older people suffer the most, since their bodies are less able to cope with harmful substances.</u> Noise from traffic and building sites is another form of pollution, which can disturb sleep and increase stress.\n\nCities can reduce pollution in several ways. Many have introduced low-emission zones that <b>restrict</b> the entry of the most polluting vehicles, and others encourage people to use public transport, bicycles or electric cars. Planting trees and creating parks also helps, because plants absorb some pollutants and make the air cleaner.\n\nMany cities have also started to publish daily air quality reports, so that residents can take precautions on bad days. Authorities hope that these reports will make people more aware of the problem. In some places, groups of citizens are measuring the pollution themselves, and <b>they</b> use the results to ask local governments for stronger action."
