        "passage": "In some cities, the bicycle is not a hobby but a normal means of transport. Visitors to Copenhagen or Amsterdam often notice that people of all ages, from schoolchildren to office workers in suits, ride bicycles to work, to school and to the shops. These cities are widely <b>regarded</b> as models for others to follow. Their success, however, did not happen by chance. It is the result of decades of planning and investment.\n\nThe key is safe infrastructure. Separate cycle lanes, protected from cars by kerbs or barriers, allow riders to feel secure. Traffic lights give cyclists their own signals, and secure parking is available at stations and shops. <u>Where such facilities are missing, many people stay away from cycling, however much they would like to try it.</u> Cities that have invested in them have seen the number of cyclists rise.\n\n[I] Cycling brings benefits to the whole community. [II] It reduces traffic and air pollution, and it keeps people healthier because they get regular exercise. [III] It also saves families money, since a bicycle costs far less than a car or a motorbike to buy and to use. [IV] Cities with many cyclists therefore need less space for roads and car parks.\n\nNot every city can copy Copenhagen or Amsterdam overnight. Many have hot weather, steep hills or narrow streets that make cycling more difficult. Even so, planners say that a city can start with a few connected cycle lanes and extend <b>them</b> year by year as more people begin to ride."
