        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Every year, many people leave rural areas to look for work in cities. Many of them are young men and women who work in factories, on construction sites or in restaurants and shops. They are often called migrant workers. Their wages are usually higher than they could earn at home, and much of the money is sent back to support families in the countryside. In this way, migrant workers make a <b>vital</b> contribution to both urban and rural economies.\n\nLife in the city is not always easy for them. Rents are high, so many workers share small rooms with several other people. Working hours can be long, and some employers do not provide written contracts or insurance. <u>Because they have moved away from home, they may also lack the support of relatives and friends in times of difficulty.</u> Their children may find it hard to enter local schools.\n\nGovernments and employers can improve the situation. Some cities have built low-cost housing for workers, and some companies provide dormitories with basic facilities. Schools can be encouraged to accept migrant children without extra fees. Moreover, training courses help workers <b>acquire</b> new skills so that they can find better jobs.\n\nSome experts argue that cities should see migrant workers as long-term residents rather than temporary visitors. After all, they have helped to build the roads, factories and houses on which the city depends. If cities offer them fair treatment, <b>they</b> are more likely to stay, to settle their families and to become active members of the community."
