        "passage": "In many cities, people are beginning to grow food in unexpected places. On rooftops, balconies and unused plots of land, residents plant vegetables, herbs and even small fruit trees. This practice, known as urban agriculture, is not entirely new, but interest in it has grown <b>rapidly</b> in recent years. One reason is that more than half of the world's population now lives in urban areas, and many city dwellers want healthier food.\n\nUrban farming offers several benefits. Fresh vegetables picked a few hours before they are eaten are tasty and nutritious, and they do not have to travel long distances, which reduces fuel use. Gardens also give residents a reason to spend time outdoors and meet their neighbours. <u>Even in small spaces, a well-planned garden can produce enough vegetables to make a real difference to a family's weekly food bill.</u> Schools that have gardens use them to teach children where food comes from.\n\n[I] Despite these benefits, urban agriculture faces several obstacles. [II] Land is expensive in cities, and owners often prefer to put up buildings rather than leave space for gardens. [III] Soil in some places may contain harmful substances from traffic or factories, so it should be tested before vegetables are planted. [IV] Water can also be a problem, since it is difficult to carry to a high roof.\n\nStill, cities are finding solutions. Some governments allow residents to use empty public land for community gardens, and companies have developed indoor farms that grow vegetables under artificial light with very little water. Experts do not expect urban farms to replace traditional farms. However, <b>they</b> can supply fresh produce close to where people live and help cities to depend less on food from far away."
