        "passage": "A park in the middle of a busy city is more than a pleasant place to walk. Public green spaces <b>enhance</b> the quality of urban life in several ways. They provide room for children to play, for older people to meet and for families to relax at weekends. For many residents of crowded areas, a park is the only place where they can find open space and fresh air.\n\nParks are also good for health. People who live near green spaces tend to exercise more, and walking among trees can reduce stress and improve mood. <u>Studies suggest that even a short visit to a park can help people to feel calmer and think more clearly.</u> Trees also reduce noise and provide shade, which is valuable on hot days.\n\nDespite these benefits, green space is not shared equally. Wealthier neighbourhoods often have more parks and tree-lined streets, while poorer districts may have very few. Where parks do exist in such districts, they are sometimes <b>neglected</b>, with broken equipment and little lighting, so people do not feel safe using them.\n\nCity planners are trying to correct this imbalance. Some set a target that every resident should live within a short walk of a park. Others turn empty land, rooftops and old railway lines into small green spaces. When local people help to design <b>them</b>, the new places are used more often and kept in better condition."
