        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Every historic city faces a difficult question: how can it keep its past while meeting the needs of the present? Old buildings often lack modern facilities such as lifts, air conditioning and reliable plumbing, and their narrow streets were not designed for cars. Developers may therefore argue that it is cheaper to <b>demolish</b> them and construct new ones. Yet once a historic building is gone, it can never be replaced.\n\nSupporters of conservation point out that heritage has real value. Historic districts attract visitors from all over the world, creating jobs in tourism, crafts and restaurants. <u>Beyond their economic value, old buildings give residents a feeling of identity by linking them to the people who lived there before.</u> Many people also find that streets with a distinctive character are more pleasant to live in than areas with identical modern blocks.\n\n[I] Many cities are now trying to combine conservation with development. [II] One method is to convert old buildings into cafés, museums, galleries or small hotels. [III] A second method is to control the design of new buildings in historic areas. [IV] Rules about height and style prevent them from spoiling the character of the neighbourhood.\n\nConservation is not always easy. It can be costly, and the owners of old houses may be unable to afford repairs. Some governments therefore offer grants or tax reductions to owners who restore their buildings in the traditional style. Experts also stress that local people should be involved, because if residents understand the value of their neighbourhood, <b>they</b> are more likely to protect it."
