        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "In many fast-growing cities, newcomers cannot afford regular housing, so they build their own homes on any land that they can find. These informal settlements, often called slums, usually grow near rivers, railways or industrial areas. The houses are often made of cheap materials such as wood, metal sheets and bricks, and many have <b>inadequate</b> access to clean water, toilets or a reliable electricity supply. Today, hundreds of millions of people around the world live in conditions like these.\n\nLife in these areas is hard in many ways. Because houses are crowded close together, diseases spread easily and fires can destroy many homes within hours. Heavy rain may cause flooding, particularly in settlements built on low-lying land. <u>Since residents often have no legal documents for their land, they live in fear that they may be forced to leave at any time.</u> This uncertainty discourages them from investing in better houses.\n\nForcing people out is rarely the answer, because they usually have nowhere else to go. Many cities now prefer to improve existing neighbourhoods, <b>supplying</b> clean water, electricity and sanitation, paving roads and giving residents legal rights to their land. Another approach is to build affordable housing close to jobs and transport so that low-income families can move into safe, regular homes.\n\nExperts say that the residents themselves should help to plan the improvements. People who have lived in a district for years know which problems are the most urgent, and projects that include local voices are more likely to succeed. Cities that learn from residents, rather than ignore <b>them</b>, can transform a crowded settlement into a safe and healthy neighbourhood."
