        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Every day, people throw away a huge amount of waste. Households, shops, offices and factories all produce rubbish, and as cities grow, so does the quantity. In many countries, most of this waste is buried in landfills, which take up large areas of land and may release harmful gases. Some of it is burned in special plants, but this can <b>emit</b> pollutants unless the plants are carefully controlled.\n\nExperts therefore recommend three steps: reduce, reuse and recycle. Reducing means producing less waste in the first place, for example by avoiding single-use plastic bags. Reusing means finding new uses for things instead of throwing them away, such as keeping food in glass jars. <u>Recycling turns used materials like paper, glass and metal into new products, which saves both raw materials and energy.</u> Of the three, reducing is usually the most effective, because waste that is never produced does not have to be managed.\n\n[I] Some cities charge residents according to the amount of waste they produce. [II] Households that sort their rubbish carefully pay less, while those that throw everything away pay more. [III] Other cities rely on education instead of fees. [IV] They run campaigns in schools and neighbourhoods to explain how to sort waste correctly.\n\nBusinesses play a part as well. Some supermarkets have stopped giving out free plastic bags, and some manufacturers now use less packaging for their products. When customers see that companies take responsibility, <b>they</b> are more likely to change their own habits."
