        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "As land becomes <b>scarce</b> and expensive, more and more people in big cities are living in high-rise buildings. A single tower can house hundreds of families, so it uses much less land than the same number of separate houses. Many modern apartment blocks also offer shared facilities such as swimming pools, gyms and playgrounds, which would be too expensive for a single family to own.\n\nLiving high above the street has other advantages. Residents often enjoy fresh air, better views and less noise from traffic. Security is also easier to provide, since entrances are guarded and cameras are installed. <u>Because many facilities are located inside or near the building, residents can save time that they would otherwise spend travelling.</u>\n\nHowever, high-rise living also has disadvantages. In tall buildings, residents depend on lifts, and a breakdown can be a serious inconvenience, especially for older people and families with small children. Children may have fewer chances to play outdoors, and neighbours often know little about one another. Some people also feel lonely when they live far above the ground, <b>detached</b> from the life of the street.\n\nArchitects are looking for ways to reduce these problems. Some new buildings include shared gardens, play areas and community rooms where residents can meet. Others are designed with shops, cafés and schools on the lower floors so that people have no need to travel far for everyday needs. Such designs show that tall buildings need not be unfriendly places if planners think about the people who live in <b>them</b>."
