        "passage": "Many large cities have grown so quickly that their centres have become overcrowded. One response is to build new towns, sometimes called satellite cities, at a distance from the centre. After the Second World War, Britain built dozens of new towns to <b>relieve</b> pressure on London and other large cities, and Japan and South Korea later created new towns around Tokyo and Seoul.\n\nA new town is planned from the beginning, so designers can avoid many of the mistakes found in old cities. Streets are laid out in an orderly pattern, land is set aside for parks and schools, and industrial zones are kept apart from housing. <u>Because everything is built at the same time, utilities such as water, electricity and drainage can be installed in a single, coordinated project.</u> Many new towns also attract companies by offering lower land prices than the main city.\n\n[I] Not every new town has succeeded. [II] Some were built far from existing jobs, and people moved there only to sleep, travelling long distances to work in the main city each day. [III] Others lacked shops, entertainment and cultural life, so residents found them dull. [IV] Planners have learnt that a new town needs jobs, services and transport links from the earliest stage.\n\nToday, many countries are building new towns again, often with the help of modern technology. Some are designed as \"smart\" towns, with sensors to manage traffic and energy. Others focus on being green, with large parks and buildings that use little energy. Experts agree that a successful new town must offer its residents a good quality of life. When <b>they</b> can find work, shops and schools within a short distance, families are more likely to stay."
