        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Cities are exciting places, but they can also be tiring. The constant noise of traffic, building work and crowds makes it hard to find peace. Noise is more than an annoyance: studies have linked long-term exposure to loud sounds with poor sleep, high blood pressure and stress. In addition, the fast pace of urban life, with its long working hours and busy streets, can leave people feeling <b>exhausted</b> and under pressure.\n\nCrowding adds to the problem. In a crowded neighbourhood, people have little privacy, and finding a quiet place to read, think or relax can be difficult. <u>Many city dwellers also say that they know few of their neighbours, which can make them feel lonely even when they are surrounded by people.</u> Some researchers believe that this sense of isolation is a major reason for the unhappiness reported in large cities.\n\nCities can take steps to reduce these pressures. Planners can separate noisy roads from housing with trees and walls, and build parks where residents can <b>unwind</b> after a busy day. Some cities limit the hours of building work or ban loud vehicles at night. Neighbourhood events, such as street festivals and community gardens, help people to get to know one another.\n\nIndividuals can also look after their own wellbeing. Taking regular walks in green spaces, limiting the use of electronic devices before bedtime and spending time with friends all help people to cope. When people make these habits part of their daily routine, <b>they</b> are better able to deal with the stress of city life."
