        "passage": "For most of the twentieth century, city planners designed streets mainly for cars. Roads were widened, car parks were built and pavements became narrower. Today, many cities are changing this approach. Streets in city centres are being closed to vehicles so that people can walk, cycle and meet freely. In Hanoi, for example, several streets around Hoan Kiem Lake are closed to traffic at weekends and become <b>vibrant</b> public spaces.\n\nCar-free streets bring many benefits. Without traffic, the air is cleaner and the noise is much lower, which makes shopping and dining outdoors more pleasant. Pedestrians are also safer, since there is no risk of being hit by a vehicle. <u>Surprisingly, shops in such areas often earn more than before, because people who walk spend more time looking at the goods than people who drive past.</u> Cities also see a rise in the number of people who walk or cycle for exercise.\n\n[I] Some shop owners fear that customers who arrive by car will stop coming. [II] Residents may worry that deliveries and emergency vehicles will be unable to reach their homes. [III] Cities usually answer these concerns by allowing delivery vehicles at fixed hours. [IV] They also provide parking just outside the car-free area, so that visitors can still arrive by car.\n\nExperience shows that opposition often fades once the benefits become visible. Some cities begin with a trial, closing a street for a few weekends and then asking residents and shop owners for their opinions. When people see how pleasant the space becomes, <b>they</b> often ask for more streets to be treated in the same way."
