        "passage": "Many cities have spread outwards over the past century, swallowing farmland and villages as they grow. This pattern, known as urban sprawl, means that homes, workplaces and shops are located far from one another. People living in sprawling areas depend heavily on cars, since public transport is difficult to provide where the population is thinly <b>scattered</b>.\n\nSprawl has several costs. Longer journeys mean more traffic, more fuel use and more air pollution. Water pipes, electricity lines and roads must be extended over greater distances, which makes services more expensive for each household. <u>As farmland and natural areas disappear under new buildings, wildlife loses its habitat and the land that once absorbed rain is covered with concrete.</u> Residents also spend less time with their families because of long journeys.\n\n[I] The alternative is the compact city, in which homes, shops, offices and schools are built close together. [II] Because everything is nearby, residents can walk or cycle for many daily activities. [III] Public transport also becomes more efficient, since many people live near each stop. [IV] Compact cities use less land, so more countryside can remain untouched.\n\nCompact cities are not without problems. If too many people are squeezed into a small area, housing becomes expensive and public spaces may feel crowded. Planners therefore stress that high density must go together with parks, playgrounds and affordable housing. If residents can enjoy such facilities, <b>they</b> are more likely to accept living in smaller homes."
