        "passage": "Cities offer jobs, education and healthcare, but these opportunities are not equally available to everyone. In many cities, wealthy districts have good schools, hospitals and parks, while poorer areas have far fewer services. The gap between rich and poor is often <b>visible</b> in the landscape itself: glass towers stand beside crowded settlements only a few streets away.\n\nTransport is a key part of this problem. Residents of poorer neighbourhoods often live far from workplaces and spend a large share of their income and time on travel. <u>If public transport is unreliable or expensive, people may be unable to take jobs or reach hospitals, even when these exist in the same city.</u> A lack of safe pavements and street lighting adds to the difficulty.\n\nCities can reduce inequality in several ways. They can invest in public transport that is cheap and frequent, and in schools and clinics in the areas that need them most. Building affordable housing near workplaces helps low-income families to save time and money. Some cities also involve residents in budget decisions, allowing communities to <b>select</b> the projects that matter most to them.\n\nExperts warn that inequality is not only unfair but also costly. When part of the population cannot find good jobs or stay healthy, the whole city loses talent and productivity. Cities with more equal access to services tend to be safer and more stable, since residents feel that <b>they</b> have a stake in the community."
