        "passage": "Not long ago, most people travelled to an office or factory every morning and returned home in the evening. Today, however, advances in technology have made it possible for millions of employees to work from home or from any place with an internet connection. This shift, known as remote working, grew rapidly in recent years and has become a <b>permanent</b> feature of many industries.\n\nRemote working offers clear advantages. Employees save the time and money they would have spent on commuting, and many find it easier to balance work with family responsibilities. Companies, for their part, can recruit talented people from different regions and sometimes reduce the cost of office space. <u>Some surveys suggest that workers who have a degree of freedom over where they work are more satisfied with their jobs than those who must be present in the office every day.</u>\n\nYet working alone at home is not without drawbacks. [I] Employees may feel isolated, since they miss the casual conversations and teamwork that happen naturally in an office. [II] The boundary between work and rest can also become unclear, and some people find themselves answering messages late at night. [III] In addition, younger workers may find it harder to learn from more experienced colleagues when they are rarely in the same room. [IV] Companies therefore need to pay attention to the well-being of their remote staff.\n\nMany organisations are therefore adopting a hybrid model, in which employees spend some days in the office and others at home. This arrangement allows people to enjoy flexibility while keeping the personal contact that supports teamwork. Employees who can combine home and office work often feel more engaged than <b>those</b> who are limited to one or the other."
