        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "For many students, the first real contact with the world of work comes through an internship or work experience placement. These short periods of training, which may last from a few weeks to several months, allow young people to try out a career before committing to it. They also give employers an opportunity to observe potential future employees.\n\nThe benefits are substantial. Interns learn how a workplace really operates, including aspects that cannot be taught in a classroom, such as dealing with deadlines or giving and receiving feedback. Students who have completed an internship often have a clearer idea of what they want to do than <b>those</b> who have not. <u>Even a placement that turns out to be disappointing can be valuable, since it helps a student to rule out a career before investing years of study in it.</u>\n\nHowever, internships are not always well organised. Some interns are given only simple tasks, such as making copies or answering the telephone, and learn very little. Others receive no payment and must rely on their families for support, which puts students from poorer backgrounds at a <b>disadvantage</b>. For this reason, experts argue that internships should have clear learning goals and, wherever possible, offer fair compensation.\n\nStudents can increase the value of an internship by preparing carefully. Researching the company beforehand, setting personal goals and asking questions during the placement all demonstrate genuine interest. At the end, thanking supervisors and asking for a reference can help to build relationships that may <b>prove</b> useful years later."
