        "passage": "When people think about finding a job, they usually imagine reading advertisements and sending applications. In reality, many positions are filled through personal contacts before they are ever advertised. This is why career experts <b>stress</b> the importance of networking, which means building and maintaining relationships with people who may offer advice, information or opportunities. A network can include classmates, teachers, former colleagues, family friends and even people you meet at events.\n\nNetworking does not mean using people. The best relationships are based on mutual benefit and honesty. <u>If you are willing to share what you know and to help others without expecting anything immediately, people will remember you when an opportunity arises.</u> Young people sometimes hesitate because they think they have nothing to offer, but enthusiasm, fresh ideas and a willingness to learn are valuable too. Simply asking an experienced person how he or she started a career can lead to a useful conversation.\n\n[I] Technology has made networking easier than ever. [II] Professional websites allow people to show their skills and experience to employers anywhere in the world. [III] However, online contacts are useful only if they are real relationships and not just names on a list. [IV] A short, polite message that explains why you want to connect is far more effective than a standard request.\n\nFinally, networking is a long-term habit rather than a quick solution. People who keep in touch, share useful information and thank those who help them will find that <b>they</b> have many friends when they need support."
