        "passage": "An apprenticeship is a form of training in which a young person learns a skilled trade by working alongside experienced professionals while also studying at a college or training centre. Apprentices are paid a wage, and they earn a <b>recognised</b> qualification at the end of the programme. Trades that traditionally use apprenticeships include carpentry, plumbing and electrical work, but today the system is also used in fields such as information technology, finance and healthcare.\n\nThe system has several advantages. Apprentices gain practical experience from the first day, which helps them to understand how theory is applied in real situations. <u>Unlike university students, who often leave with large debts, apprentices usually finish their training without having to pay for it.</u> Employers also benefit, since they can train workers in exactly the skills that their companies need.\n\nHowever, apprenticeships also face problems. In some countries, young people and their parents still believe that a university degree is the only <b>route</b> to a good career, so apprenticeships are seen as a second choice. Some employers offer poor training, using apprentices as cheap labour instead of helping them to learn real skills.\n\nGovernments can improve the system by setting clear standards for training and by checking that companies follow them. Some countries also run campaigns to change attitudes, showing successful people who began as apprentices. When young people see that <b>they</b> can build a rewarding career without a degree, more of them are likely to choose this path."
