        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Few jobs today can be done entirely alone. Whether in a hospital, a factory or an office, people usually have to cooperate to reach shared goals. Employers therefore put great <b>emphasis</b> on teamwork and often ask interview candidates to describe times when they worked successfully in a group.\n\nA good team has several features. Members share a clear goal, and each person understands his or her role. They communicate openly, listening to one another and discussing disagreements calmly. <u>Instead of blaming individuals for mistakes, members of effective teams look for ways to prevent the same problem from happening again.</u> Leaders play an important part by encouraging everyone to express an opinion.\n\n[I] Teamwork, however, does not always run smoothly. [II] Some members may do less than their share and rely on others to finish the task. [III] Strong personalities may dominate discussions, so quieter members never get the chance to share useful ideas. [IV] Such problems can damage the mood of the whole group and slow down the project.\n\nFortunately, these difficulties can be reduced. Teams that set clear rules from the start, divide tasks fairly and hold short regular meetings usually work better. Some companies also train staff in communication and conflict resolution so that <b>they</b> know how to deal with disagreements."
