        "passage": "In many workplaces, long hours are seen as a sign of commitment. Employees who stay late at the office are often admired, while those who leave on time may be thought less <b>dedicated</b>. However, researchers who have studied working patterns suggest that working longer does not always mean working better. After a certain number of hours, people become tired, make more mistakes and find it harder to concentrate.\n\nSome companies have tested shorter working weeks. In these experiments, employees worked fewer hours but were paid the same salary. <u>Many of the companies reported that productivity stayed the same or even rose, while staff said that they felt healthier and less stressed.</u> Several firms decided to keep the shorter week after the trial ended.\n\nCritics, however, point out that a shorter week does not suit every job. Hospitals, restaurants and shops need staff to be present at fixed times, and it can be difficult to <b>reduce</b> hours without hiring extra workers. In some fast-growing companies, employees also worry that they will not have enough time to complete their tasks, and so they work just as hard in four days as they did in five.\n\nExperts say the key is to focus on results rather than hours. Managers who set clear goals, trust their staff and allow flexibility often find that <b>they</b> get better work from their teams."
