        "passage": "Technology is changing jobs faster than ever before. Some jobs, such as those of bank tellers and typists, have become less common, while new jobs, such as app developers and data analysts, did not exist a few decades ago. Experts say that many workers will need to <b>update</b> their skills several times during their careers. To describe this process, they use two terms: upskilling, which means learning new skills for the same job, and reskilling, which means learning the skills needed for a completely different one.\n\nCompanies have good reasons to support this process. Hiring a new employee is expensive, and training existing staff costs less than finding replacements. <u>Workers who are offered opportunities to learn also tend to be more loyal, since they feel that their employer values their future.</u> Many companies therefore provide online courses, workshops and even paid study time.\n\n[I] Workers themselves must also take responsibility for their development. [II] They can start by identifying which skills are becoming more valuable in their field. [III] Reading industry news, talking to colleagues and following online courses all help to reveal these trends. [IV] Even twenty minutes of study a day can add up to a great deal over a year.\n\nEmployers who encourage staff to learn from one another often find that <b>they</b> build stronger teams. For example, younger employees can teach digital tools to older colleagues, who in turn share their experience of dealing with customers."
