        "passage": "Volunteering means giving your time and effort to help others without being paid. Millions of people around the world volunteer every year, working in hospitals, schools, animal shelters, disaster areas and many other places. Some do it once or twice a year, while others give several hours every week. Their motives <b>vary</b>: some want to help a cause they care about, some wish to meet new people, and others hope to gain skills for their careers.\n\nVolunteering can also help with a career. Many volunteers say that they have developed skills such as communication, leadership and problem-solving. <u>Because these abilities are hard to learn from books, employers often regard voluntary experience as almost as valuable as paid work.</u> Volunteering can also lead directly to job offers, as organisations may hire people who have already proved themselves.\n\n[I] However, volunteering is not without difficulties. [II] Some volunteers are given tasks that do not match their skills, and they soon lose interest. [III] Others commit to more than they can manage and become exhausted. [IV] Good organisations avoid these problems by explaining the role clearly and by thanking volunteers for their work.\n\nAnyone who wants to start can look for a local organisation, such as a youth union, school club or charity. Beginners should take small steps, trying a project for a month or two before deciding whether to continue. Those who find an activity that suits <b>them</b> are more likely to keep volunteering for years."
