        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "More and more people are choosing to work in other countries. Some are sent abroad by their companies, while others move on their own in search of better pay, new experiences or opportunities that are not available at home. Working abroad can be exciting, but it also requires <b>courage</b>, because employees must adapt to a new language, a new climate and a different working culture.\n\nDifferences in working culture can cause misunderstandings. In some countries, employees address their managers by their first names and freely express disagreement; in others, such behaviour would be considered disrespectful. <u>Even small matters, such as punctuality or the way in which a request is made, may carry different meanings from one culture to another.</u> Newcomers who recognise these differences are less likely to offend colleagues by accident.\n\nLanguage is another challenge. Even employees who speak the local language well may struggle with technical vocabulary, jokes or regional accents. Many companies therefore offer language classes and cultural training before their staff <b>depart</b>. Colleagues can also help by speaking clearly and being patient with newcomers.\n\nDespite these difficulties, many people find that working abroad changes them for the better. They become more independent, learn to see problems from different viewpoints and gain confidence. When they return home, <b>they</b> often bring useful ideas and international contacts that benefit their employers."
