        "passage": "Every team needs a leader, but leaders do not all behave in the same way. Researchers who study management often describe three main styles. An authoritarian leader makes decisions alone and expects others to follow instructions. A democratic leader invites team members to share ideas before a decision is made. A laissez-faire leader gives the team great freedom and <b>intervenes</b> only when necessary.\n\nNo single style is best in every situation. An authoritarian approach can be useful in an emergency, when there is no time for discussion. A democratic style often produces better ideas and greater commitment, since people support decisions that they helped to make. <u>Laissez-faire leadership works well with experienced, self-motivated professionals but may leave less confident workers without the guidance they need.</u> Skilled leaders therefore adapt their style to the people and the task.\n\n[I] Leaders also differ in how well they understand emotions. [II] Those with high emotional intelligence recognise their own feelings and control them, even under pressure. [III] They also pay attention to the feelings of others and respond with understanding. [IV] Such leaders often build teams in which members trust one another and feel safe to admit mistakes.\n\nThe good news is that leadership skills can be learnt. Many companies run training programmes in which managers practise listening, giving feedback and handling conflict. Young people can start even earlier by taking responsibility in school clubs and volunteer groups, where <b>they</b> can try different styles and see which one works best."
