        "passage": "In many countries, young people are more likely to be unemployed than older workers. Even in economies that are growing, school-leavers and graduates may spend months searching for their first job. The reasons are not hard to understand. Employers usually prefer candidates with work experience, but young people cannot gain experience without being given a job. In addition, companies tend to <b>recruit</b> fewer new staff during periods of economic difficulty.\n\nAnother cause is a mismatch between education and the labour market. In some fields, there are far more graduates than vacancies, while in others employers cannot find enough qualified people. <u>Young people who choose their studies without checking which skills are in demand may therefore find that their qualifications do not lead to the jobs they hoped for.</u> This is particularly true when courses are not updated to reflect changes in technology.\n\nUnemployment has serious effects on young people. Beyond the loss of income, a long period without work can reduce confidence and make it harder to find a job later, because employers may question gaps in a CV. Some young people become <b>discouraged</b> and stop looking for work altogether.\n\nGovernments and employers can help in several ways. Career guidance in schools helps students to choose subjects wisely, while apprenticeships and internships give young people the experience that employers want. Some countries also offer financial support to companies that hire young workers, so that <b>they</b> are more willing to take the risk of employing someone without experience."
