        "passage": "Germany is often cited as a model of vocational education. A large share of young people there do not go to university but instead follow a \"dual system\" of training, in which they spend part of the week in a company and part in a vocational school. Apprentices are paid a modest salary, and the training usually lasts between two and three and a half years. At the end, they take an exam that is recognised throughout the country, so their qualifications are easy to <b>transfer</b> from one employer to another.\n\nThe system has several strengths. Because companies help to design the courses, training matches the skills that employers actually need. <u>Young people who complete an apprenticeship often move straight into a job, sometimes with the same company that trained them.</u> As a result, unemployment among young people in Germany has generally been lower than in many other European countries.\n\n[I] Other countries have tried to copy the model, but success depends on cooperation. [II] Companies must be willing to take on apprentices and invest time in teaching them. [III] Schools and employers must work closely together so that courses stay up to date. [IV] Above all, society must respect vocational qualifications as much as university degrees.\n\nInterest in this approach is growing elsewhere too. In Vietnam, for example, the government has encouraged vocational schools to cooperate with businesses so that students can practise in real workplaces. If graduates find good jobs quickly, <b>they</b> will help to change the image of vocational education."
