        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "What makes people enjoy their work? Many people assume that a high salary is the most important factor, and money certainly matters, especially when it is too low to meet basic needs. However, studies of workers in many countries suggest that, once their basic needs are met, other factors become more <b>significant</b>. These include interesting tasks, a sense of progress, good relationships with colleagues and the feeling that one's work has a purpose.\n\nAutonomy is particularly valued. Employees who can decide how to do their work, rather than following every step of a fixed procedure, tend to be more creative and committed. <u>Being trusted with responsibility sends a message that the employee's judgment is respected, which in turn increases motivation.</u> Recognition also plays a role: a simple word of thanks from a manager can make staff feel valued.\n\nOn the other hand, certain conditions reduce satisfaction. Heavy workloads, unclear instructions and a lack of fairness in the way promotions are decided can make employees feel <b>frustrated</b>. Over time, such feelings lead to absence, low performance and a high rate of people leaving the company.\n\nCompanies that understand these factors can create better workplaces. Some ask employees for regular feedback and act on it, while others offer training and clear career paths. When workers see that managers listen and care, <b>they</b> are more likely to stay and to recommend the company to others."
