        "passage": "In the past, many people chose one career at the age of twenty and stayed in it until they retired. Today, this pattern is becoming less common. Longer working lives, rapid technological change and a greater willingness to <b>pursue</b> personal interests have encouraged more adults to change careers, sometimes more than once.\n\nPeople change careers for different reasons. Some lose their jobs because their industry is shrinking, while others feel bored or stressed and want a new challenge. <u>A growing number are also motivated by a wish to do work that matches their values, such as helping others or protecting the environment.</u> Whatever the reason, a change of career requires careful thought.\n\nChanging careers is not without difficulty. Adults who move into a new field may have to accept a lower salary at first, since they lack experience in it. They may also need to study again, which takes time and money. In addition, some employers are <b>reluctant</b> to hire older beginners, although others value the maturity and life experience that they bring.\n\nFortunately, there are ways to reduce the risk. Experts suggest testing a new field before leaving a secure job, for instance by taking a short course, doing voluntary work or talking to people who already work in it. Skills such as management, communication and problem-solving can often be transferred from one career to another, so adults should remember that <b>they</b> are not starting from zero."
