        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Recent advances in artificial intelligence have made it possible to create realistic images, voices and videos of events that never happened. Such material, known as deepfakes, can be entertaining when used in films or jokes. Yet it also makes it easier to spread false information, because many viewers find it hard to tell real recordings from artificial ones.\n\nThe consequences can be serious. A fake video of a public figure can damage a reputation or influence opinion before the truth is established. Fraudsters have copied people's voices to trick their relatives into sending money. People who regularly check the source of a story tend to be less easily deceived than <b>those</b> who share content without thinking. <u>Because false material can spread within minutes, the damage is often done long before a correction appears.</u>\n\nTechnology companies and governments are responding. Some platforms now label AI-generated content, and researchers are developing tools that can detect signs of artificial editing. Several countries are preparing laws that would make it illegal to use such material to <b>deceive</b> or harm others. However, creators of false content often find new methods faster than detectors can keep up.\n\nOrdinary people can protect themselves with a few habits. They can pause before sharing <b>startling</b> content, compare it with reports from reliable sources and look for unnatural details such as strange shadows or movements. Schools can help by teaching media literacy as a core skill, so that young people learn to question what they see instead of simply accepting it."
