        "passage": "Hospitals produce enormous quantities of data, from X-rays and scans to blood tests and patient records. Artificial intelligence is well suited to finding patterns in such data. In some studies, computer programs have been able to identify certain cancers in medical images with an accuracy comparable to that of experienced specialists. Such tools are not intended to replace doctors, but to act as a second pair of eyes that can <b>flag</b> suspicious cases for closer examination.\n\nAI also helps with patient care outside the hospital. Smartwatches can monitor heart rate and warn users of irregular rhythms, and apps can remind patients to take their medicine. <u>For people living far from hospitals, programs that analyse symptoms can offer advice on whether a visit to a doctor is necessary.</u> In some countries, doctors even use video calls combined with AI tools to examine patients in remote villages.\n\n[I] Despite these benefits, medical AI has limits. [II] A program is only as good as the data used to train it, and if that data comes mostly from one group of people, the results may be less accurate for others. [III] Patients' records are also highly private, so hospitals must store them securely. [IV] Doctors and patients need to understand how a tool reaches its conclusions before they can trust it.\n\nMany experts therefore describe AI as an assistant rather than a replacement. A radiologist, for example, may spend less time on routine scans and more time talking with patients. If hospitals introduce the technology carefully, <b>they</b> can improve both the speed and the quality of care."
