        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Artificial intelligence is often described as objective, because it relies on numbers rather than feelings. In reality, however, an AI system can be unfair. This happens because programs learn from data created by humans, and human society contains <b>prejudices</b>. If the examples used to train a system reflect old patterns of unfairness, the system may repeat them.\n\nSeveral cases have shown the problem. In one well-known case, a recruitment program that learned from the records of past employees, most of whom were men, began to prefer male applicants. Some face recognition systems were found to make more mistakes with darker skin than with lighter skin. <u>Neither of these systems was designed to discriminate, but both produced unfair results because the data they learned from was unbalanced.</u>\n\nExperts suggest several ways of reducing bias. Developers can test their systems on many different groups of people before releasing them, and they can collect more varied data. Some companies employ independent teams to <b>audit</b> their programs, checking whether the results are fair. Laws are also being proposed in several countries that would require organisations to explain automated decisions that affect people's lives.\n\nFairness, however, is not only a technical question. People must decide what counts as fair, and different groups may disagree. For this reason, many experts argue that ordinary citizens, not just engineers, should take part in the discussion. When people feel that their views have been heard, <b>they</b> are more likely to trust the technology."
