        "passage": "Self-driving cars, also called autonomous vehicles, are designed to travel with little or no human control. They use cameras, radar and other sensors to <b>detect</b> other vehicles, road signs and pedestrians, and artificial intelligence software processes this information to decide when to steer, speed up or brake. Engineers often describe levels of automation, from simple driver-assistance features such as automatic braking to vehicles that can drive themselves in certain conditions.\n\nSupporters of the technology point to safety. Most road accidents are caused at least partly by human error, such as tiredness, distraction or driving too fast. <u>Because a computer never gets tired or looks at a phone, self-driving cars could reduce the number of crashes if the technology works reliably.</u> They might also help elderly people and those with disabilities to travel more independently.\n\n[I] However, the technology still faces serious challenges. [II] Sensors can struggle in heavy rain, fog or snow, and software may not always know how to react to unusual situations, such as a road worker waving traffic through. [III] There is also the question of responsibility: if a driverless car causes an accident, it is not clear whether the owner, the manufacturer or the software company should be blamed. [IV] Laws in many countries have not yet been fully adapted to such cases.\n\nDespite these problems, car makers and technology companies continue to test driverless vehicles on public roads, often with a safety driver ready to take control. Many experts expect fully autonomous cars to appear first in limited areas, such as city centres or fixed bus routes. Whether <b>they</b> will spread beyond these areas depends not only on engineering but also on public trust and clear laws."
