        "passage": "In 2018, a portrait created by an artificial intelligence system was sold at an auction house in New York for more than 400,000 US dollars. The sale surprised many people and <b>sparked</b> a worldwide debate about whether machines can create art. Since then, image generators that turn written descriptions into pictures have become available to the public, and millions of people now use them for fun, for work or for study.\n\nSupporters say that AI tools make art more accessible. A person who cannot draw can still turn an idea into an image, and professional designers can test many versions of a design in a few minutes. <u>Some artists see the software as a new kind of brush: it does not replace creativity but gives it a different form.</u> Others use it to explore styles that would take years to master by hand.\n\n[I] Critics, however, point out that the technology raises difficult legal questions. [II] Image generators learn by studying enormous collections of existing pictures, many of which were made by living artists who never gave permission. [III] Several artists have taken legal action against AI companies, arguing that their work was used unfairly. [IV] In the United States, the Copyright Office has stated that images produced entirely by AI, without human creative input, cannot be protected by copyright.\n\nNot everyone is pessimistic. Some lawyers believe that clear rules can be developed, for example by requiring companies to be open about the data they use or by paying artists whose work is included. Meanwhile, many creators are learning to work with the technology rather than against it, hoping that <b>they</b> can keep control over how their style is used."
