        "passage": "A smart home is a house in which devices such as lights, heating, locks and cameras are connected to the internet and can be controlled from a phone or by voice. Many of these devices use artificial intelligence to learn the habits of the people living there. For example, a smart thermostat can <b>adjust</b> the temperature automatically when it notices that nobody is at home.\n\nThe main attraction of smart homes is convenience. Residents can switch off lights from their beds, check who is at the door while they are at work, or ask a speaker to play music without touching anything. <u>Smart devices can also help families to save money, because they can reduce the use of electricity and water when these are not needed.</u> For elderly people, sensors can even alert relatives if someone falls.\n\nHowever, smart homes bring risks. Every connected device collects information, such as when people wake up, when they leave and which rooms they use. If this data is not <b>protected</b> properly, hackers could misuse it, and some devices have been found to have weak security. Moreover, a house that depends on the internet may stop working normally when the connection fails.\n\nExperts therefore advise buyers to choose products from reputable companies, to change default passwords and to install updates regularly. Updates matter because manufacturers stop supporting old devices after some years, and once that happens <b>they</b> may become easy targets for hackers. Users should therefore check how long a product will be supported before buying it."
