        "passage": "Agriculture has always depended on knowledge: when to plant, how much water to give and how to protect crops from pests. Today, artificial intelligence is helping farmers to make these decisions with greater <b>precision</b>. Sensors placed in the soil measure moisture and nutrients, drones take pictures of fields from above, and software analyses the data to show which areas need attention.\n\nThese tools can bring clear benefits. A system that knows exactly which plants are thirsty can water only those plants, so less water is wasted. <u>Cameras linked to AI software can recognise early signs of disease on leaves, which allows farmers to treat a problem before it spreads across the whole field.</u> As a result, farmers may use smaller amounts of chemicals and still protect their harvest.\n\n[I] Robots are also beginning to appear on farms. [II] Some machines can pick ripe strawberries or tomatoes without damaging them, which is useful because workers are hard to find in many regions. [III] Other machines move along rows of vegetables and remove weeds, so fewer chemicals are needed. [IV] Most of these machines are still expensive, and they work best in orderly environments such as greenhouses.\n\nEven so, AI is not a magic solution. Small-scale farmers may find the equipment too expensive, and in some areas the internet connection is too weak to send data. Experts therefore recommend that governments and companies offer low-cost tools and training. Without such support, <b>they</b> may be left behind as larger farms modernise."
