        "passage": "Machine translation has improved dramatically in recent years. Early programs translated texts word by word, which often produced sentences that were <b>awkward</b> or even impossible to understand. Modern systems use neural networks that learn from millions of examples of human translation, and as a result, they can take the whole sentence into account.\n\nToday, translation apps are used by travellers, students and businesses. A tourist can point a phone camera at a menu and read it in another language, while a company can translate its website into dozens of languages in a few minutes. <u>Real-time translation tools can even let two people who speak different languages hold a conversation with only a short delay.</u> For languages with a lot of online text, such as English, Spanish or Chinese, the results are usually good.\n\nHowever, machines are still far from perfect. Languages with little online text, such as many minority languages, are translated much less accurately because the software has fewer examples to learn from. Jokes, idioms and cultural references are also <b>problematic</b>: a phrase that is clear to a native speaker may be translated literally and lose its meaning. In important areas such as law and medicine, mistakes can have serious consequences.\n\nFor this reason, professional translators are not disappearing. Many of them now use machine translation to produce a first draft and then correct it, which saves time. Translation apps are useful tools, but teachers advise learners not to depend on <b>them</b> completely, since they sometimes produce errors that only a skilled reader can notice."
