        "passage": "Chatbots such as ChatGPT can answer questions, write essays and even produce computer code. They are based on large language models, which are trained on <b>vast</b> amounts of text from books, websites and other sources. During training, the model learns which words are likely to come next in a sentence, and this allows it to produce answers that sound natural.\n\nMany students and workers already use these tools. They can summarise long documents, suggest ideas for a project or help a learner to practise a foreign language. <u>Because they respond in everyday language, even people with no technical knowledge can use them without special training.</u> Companies are also adding chatbots to their websites to answer customers' questions at any time of day.\n\n[I] They do not truly understand the text they produce; instead, they predict which words are likely to come next, and sometimes they state false information in a confident tone. [II] Researchers call such mistakes \"hallucinations\". [III] The answers may also reflect biases found in the texts on which the system was trained. [IV] For this reason, important facts, such as medical or legal advice, should always be checked against reliable sources.\n\nSchools and universities are still deciding how to respond. Some teachers allow students to use chatbots for brainstorming ideas but not for writing final answers, while others ask students to explain their work in person. Educators agree that students should learn to question what <b>they</b> read, whether it comes from a person or a machine."
