        "passage": "Climate change is one of the biggest challenges of our time, and scientists are looking to artificial intelligence for help. AI can analyse satellite images to monitor forests and show where trees are being cut down illegally. It can also improve weather forecasts, which helps farmers and cities to <b>prepare</b> for floods, droughts and storms.\n\nEnergy is another area where AI is useful. Electricity networks have to balance supply and demand every second, and <u>software that predicts when sunshine and wind will be strong can help operators to use more renewable energy and burn less fuel.</u> In buildings, smart systems can switch off heating and lights in empty rooms, which saves energy and money.\n\nYet AI also has an environmental cost. Training large models requires powerful computers that run for weeks, and data centres, where the models are stored and used, consume a great deal of electricity and water for cooling. According to some estimates, data centres already use one or two per cent of the world's electricity, and this share is expected to <b>grow</b> as AI spreads.\n\nResearchers are developing more efficient models, and some governments are encouraging the use of renewable energy in computing. Critics add that technology companies should publish figures about the energy <b>they</b> use, so that the public can judge whether AI is worth its cost."
