        "passage": "Natural disasters such as typhoons, floods and earthquakes cause great damage every year, and countries like Vietnam, which experience many typhoons, know how important early warnings are. Traditional weather forecasts rely on <b>complex</b> mathematical models that run on powerful supercomputers. These models are accurate, but they require enormous amounts of computing power and time.\n\nArtificial intelligence offers another approach. Instead of solving equations, AI systems learn patterns from decades of recorded weather data. <u>Some AI models have matched or even outperformed traditional systems on many measures while using far less computing power, which means forecasts can be produced more quickly and cheaply.</u> Faster forecasts give people more time to prepare.\n\n[I] AI is also being tested for other kinds of disasters. [II] For floods, systems that study rainfall, river levels and satellite images can estimate which areas are likely to be under water several days ahead. [III] In the case of earthquakes, AI can help scientists to find very small tremors in seismic records, but it cannot yet predict exactly when and where a big earthquake will strike. [IV] Scientists stress that no technology can remove the risk completely.\n\nEven a perfect forecast saves lives only if it reaches people in time and they know what to do. Many countries therefore combine technology with community training: villagers practise evacuation drills, and warnings are sent by mobile phone, radio and loudspeakers. When local people trust the warnings and understand <b>them</b>, they are far more likely to leave dangerous areas quickly."
