        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "A deepfake is a video, image or audio recording that has been created or changed with artificial intelligence to show someone doing or saying something that never <b>happened</b>. The technology learns from many photos and recordings of a real person, and it can then produce new material that looks and sounds very convincing.\n\nDeepfakes are not always harmful. Film studios use similar techniques to make actors look younger or to translate dialogue into other languages, and museums have used them to bring historical figures to life for visitors. <u>The same tools, however, can be used to deceive people, for example by creating a fake video of a politician or a false voice message from a relative asking for money.</u> Such scams have already caused financial losses in several countries.\n\nBecause deepfakes spread quickly on social media, they can <b>damage</b> reputations and make people doubt real evidence. Detection is difficult: the software improves so fast that tips such as 'look for unnatural blinking' soon become out of date. Researchers are building tools that look for tiny technical traces of editing, but the creators of fake content keep finding ways around them.\n\nWhat can ordinary users do? Experts advise them to check where a video comes from and whether trusted news organisations have reported it. Some platforms now label AI-generated content, and several countries are preparing laws that require such material to be marked clearly. Experts also warn that shocking videos deserve extra care, since the people who create them often hope that viewers will share <b>them</b> before checking the facts."
