        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Many modern AI systems, especially those based on deep neural networks, are often described as 'black boxes'. They produce an answer, such as approving a loan or recommending a medical treatment, but even their designers may find it hard to explain exactly how that answer was <b>reached</b>. This is because the systems learn from millions of examples and adjust countless internal settings that no human can examine one by one.\n\nLack of transparency matters most when decisions change people's lives. If a bank refuses a loan or a company rejects a job application, the person concerned has a reasonable wish to know why. <u>Without an explanation, it is almost impossible for them to challenge a mistake or to find out whether the decision was unfair.</u> Doctors, too, are reluctant to follow a treatment recommendation that they cannot understand.\n\n[I] Researchers are therefore developing 'explainable AI'. [II] Some methods show which pieces of information had the greatest influence on a decision, for example by highlighting the part of an X-ray image that led a program to suspect a disease. [III] Others use simpler models that are easier to follow, even if they are slightly less powerful. [IV] However, explanations produced by such tools are sometimes incomplete, and experts disagree about how much detail is needed.\n\nPolicy makers are also taking action. The European Union, for instance, has introduced rules that place stricter requirements on AI used in areas such as hiring and lending, including human oversight of important decisions. Supporters believe such rules will encourage companies to design systems that people can trust. Critics reply that rules should not be so strict that <b>they</b> prevent useful innovation."
