        "passage": "The idea of machines that can think is older than the computer. In 1950, the British mathematician Alan Turing published a famous paper that asked whether machines could think, and he <b>proposed</b> a test in which a person talks to both a human and a machine without knowing which is which. In 1956, a group of researchers met at a workshop in the United States, where the term 'artificial intelligence' was first used.\n\nEarly progress was fast, but it did not last. Programs of the 1950s and 1960s could solve simple puzzles and play checkers, yet they failed at tasks that people find easy, such as recognising a face. <u>When governments and companies saw that results were far below the promises that scientists had made, they reduced funding, and interest in the field fell sharply for several years.</u> Such periods are sometimes called 'AI winters'.\n\n[I] Interest returned in the 1990s, when computers became much faster. [II] In 1997, an IBM computer called Deep Blue defeated the world chess champion Garry Kasparov in a match. [III] In 2016, a program called AlphaGo defeated Lee Sedol, one of the world's strongest Go players, by four games to one. [IV] Around the same time, deep learning, which uses neural networks with many layers, began to transform speech and image recognition.\n\nToday's AI is far more capable than its early ancestors, but it remains narrow: a program that plays Go brilliantly cannot answer a medical question or drive a car. Some experts predict that machines will eventually match human intelligence in general. Others are more cautious and point out that today's systems do not truly understand the world; <b>they</b> only find patterns in data."
