        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "As artificial intelligence takes over more routine tasks, a question arises: which human abilities will remain most valuable? Surveys of employers by international organisations regularly place analytical thinking, creativity and flexibility near the top of the list. These are skills that machines can imitate only in <b>limited</b> ways, and they are useful in almost every occupation.\n\nCreativity is often the first example that people mention. AI can generate thousands of designs in a few seconds, but it does not know which of them will move people or solve a real problem. <u>Human creators decide what is worth making, and they bring personal experience and cultural understanding that software lacks.</u> For this reason, many companies value employees who can suggest original ideas as well as use digital tools.\n\nSocial skills are equally important. Teamwork, communication and empathy are needed whenever people must <b>cooperate</b> to reach a goal, and machines can only simulate them. A nurse who calmly explains a treatment, or a manager who resolves a quarrel between colleagues, is doing work that depends on understanding emotions, not just processing information.\n\nNone of these abilities develops overnight. Students can practise them by working on group projects, taking part in debates and volunteering in the community, activities in which <b>they</b> must solve problems with real people. Teachers and parents can help by giving young people chances to try things, make mistakes and learn from them, rather than expecting perfect results at once."
