        "passage": "Not long ago, most people learned about world events from newspapers, radio and television. Today, a growing number of them, especially young people, get their news from social media platforms. News stories appear between photographs of friends and videos of pets, and they reach readers within seconds of being posted. This change has made information more <b>accessible</b>, but it has also altered how news is chosen and judged.\n\nOn social media, stories are not selected by editors but by algorithms, which show users the content that they are most likely to read or share. As a result, two people may see very different versions of the same event. <u>People who only meet opinions similar to their own may come to believe that everybody agrees with them, and this makes honest debate more difficult.</u> Researchers sometimes call this effect a filter bubble.\n\n[I] Social media has also changed who can act as a reporter. [II] Eyewitnesses can post photographs and videos of events as they happen, often before professional journalists arrive. [III] This can reveal information that might otherwise remain hidden. [IV] On the other hand, such material is rarely checked, and mistakes or deliberate lies can spread quickly.\n\nBecause of these risks, experts advise readers to treat social media as one source among many. Reading the full article instead of the headline, checking who published a story and comparing it with established news outlets take only a few minutes, yet <b>they</b> greatly reduce the chance of being misled."
