        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Fake news is false or misleading information that is presented as if it were real news. It is not a new phenomenon, but the internet has made it much easier to produce and <b>circulate</b>. Anyone can create a convincing website or video in a few hours, and with a single click, a false story can be sent to thousands of people.\n\nWhy do people believe and share false stories? One reason is that they often look interesting or shocking, and strong emotions make people react quickly. Another reason is that people tend to trust information that agrees with their own opinions. <u>A study of Twitter by researchers at MIT found that false stories were about 70 per cent more likely to be shared than true ones.</u> Information from friends and family also seems more trustworthy than information from strangers.\n\n[I] Fake news can have serious consequences. [II] During the COVID-19 pandemic, false claims about cures and vaccines spread widely online, and some people may have made harmful choices as a result. [III] Fake stories can also damage the reputation of innocent people or cause panic during natural disasters. [IV] In the long term, they may reduce public trust in all news, including reports that are accurate.\n\nFortunately, there are simple steps that readers can take. First, check who published the story and whether other trusted outlets are reporting the same facts. Second, look at the date and the evidence, as old photographs are often used to illustrate new events. Many schools now teach these skills in media literacy lessons, so that students are able to question the posts that <b>they</b> see online."
