        "passage": "When you open a social media app, the posts you see are not chosen at random. They are selected by algorithms, sets of rules that <b>predict</b> what you will find interesting. The system studies what you have liked, watched and shared in the past, and then offers more content of the same kind. This is one reason why many people find it hard to put down their phones.\n\nPersonalisation has clear benefits. You can discover new music, find tutorials on subjects you are learning or hear from people who share your hobbies. <u>Without such filtering, the huge amount of content published every second would be impossible for any individual to sort through.</u> For companies, it also means that advertisements can be shown to the people who are most likely to be interested.\n\n[I] There is, however, a downside. [II] Because algorithms keep offering content similar to what you have already seen, you may rarely meet ideas that challenge your views. [III] Researchers describe this situation as an 'echo chamber', in which people hear mostly opinions that match their own. [IV] Some studies suggest, however, that the effect is weaker than many people fear, because most users also see content from other sources.\n\nWhat can users do? Experts suggest following a variety of sources, including some with different viewpoints, and adjusting the settings of apps to reduce personalised content. Some platforms now allow users to choose a chronological feed, which shows posts in the order in which <b>they</b> were published. Above all, people should stay curious and ask themselves why a particular post has appeared on their screens."
