        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Cyberbullying means using digital technology to hurt, frighten or embarrass another person. It can take many forms, from sending cruel messages to spreading rumours or sharing private photographs without permission. Unlike traditional bullying, it can follow the victim home, because the messages arrive on phones and computers at any hour of the day. It is also <b>persistent</b>: once something has been posted online, it may stay there for years.\n\nThe effects can be serious. Victims often feel anxious, lonely or ashamed, and some lose interest in school or in activities that they previously enjoyed. <u>Because the attackers can hide behind false names, they may feel less responsible for their actions and behave more cruelly than they would face to face.</u> Bystanders who see harmful posts but say nothing may also make the situation worse.\n\n[I] Social media companies have a part to play in solving the problem. [II] Most platforms now offer tools that allow users to block accounts, report harmful posts and limit who can comment on their content. [III] In addition, some use artificial intelligence to detect <b>abusive</b> language and warn users before they post a hurtful message. [IV] Critics say, however, that these measures are not always applied consistently, and that harmful content sometimes stays online for too long.\n\nSchools, parents and young people themselves can also help. Many schools run awareness programmes that teach students to treat others with respect online. Parents are advised to talk openly with their children, who may hide problems because <b>they</b> fear that their phones will be taken away. Victims should save the evidence, avoid replying angrily and tell a trusted adult as soon as possible."
