        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Citizen journalism refers to news gathering and reporting by ordinary people rather than by professional reporters. Thanks to smartphones, almost anyone can record an event, take photographs and share them online within seconds. When something <b>unforeseen</b> happens, such as an earthquake, a flood or a street accident, eyewitnesses are often on the scene long before a professional crew can arrive.\n\nCitizen journalism has brought real benefits. During some natural disasters, photographs and videos posted by local people have helped rescue teams to find areas that needed urgent help. <u>Ordinary people can also draw attention to problems that traditional media may overlook, such as damaged roads in a small village or unsafe working conditions in a local factory.</u> In countries where the press is not free, such reports may be the only way for people to learn what is happening.\n\n[I] However, citizen journalism also has weaknesses. [II] Unlike professional reporters, ordinary people usually have no training in checking facts, so mistakes can easily occur. [III] For example, a photograph may be old, edited or taken in a different place, and a witness may see only a small part of a larger event. [IV] Moreover, there is no editor to decide whether a video is fair or whether it harms the privacy of the people shown in it.\n\nMany news organisations now try to combine the strengths of both approaches. Some invite readers to send in photographs and videos, which journalists then verify before publishing. Others train volunteers in basic reporting skills. Experts agree that audiences, too, have a role to play: when people see a dramatic video online, <b>they</b> should ask who recorded it, when and where, before deciding whether to believe it."
