        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Over the last twenty years, many magazines have closed or moved online. Advertisers have <b>shifted</b> their money to websites and social media, and young readers increasingly look at articles on screens rather than on paper.\n\nYet printed magazines have not disappeared. Some specialist titles, such as those about photography, cooking or travel, have loyal readers who enjoy the quality of the paper and the beauty of large photographs. <u>For these readers, holding a magazine is a pleasant experience that a screen cannot completely replace, and they are often willing to pay more for it.</u> Printed magazines can also be shared, collected and kept for many years.\n\nResearch also suggests that the format may affect how we read. Some studies have found that people understand long and complicated texts slightly better on paper than on screens, perhaps because screens encourage faster, more <b>superficial</b> reading and offer many distractions. Other studies, however, show no clear difference, so the question is still being debated.\n\nFor publishers, the challenge is to find a balance. Many now offer both printed and digital editions, and some use their websites to attract readers who may later buy the paper version. Readers, meanwhile, increasingly choose the format that suits the situation: <b>they</b> may read a news summary on a phone during a journey but enjoy a printed magazine at the weekend."
