        "passage": "The first great revolution in mass communication came in the fifteenth century, when Johannes Gutenberg introduced a printing press with movable metal type in Europe around 1440. Before that, books had to be copied by hand, which was slow and expensive. Printing made it possible to produce many <b>identical</b> copies quickly, and by the early seventeenth century, the first regular newspapers had appeared.\n\nThe twentieth century brought electronic media. Radio broadcasting began to reach homes in the 1920s, allowing people to hear news, music and speeches at the same moment, even if they lived hundreds of kilometres apart. <u>Television, which became common in many countries after the Second World War, added moving pictures and quickly became the main source of news and entertainment for millions of families.</u> Both media were one-way: audiences could listen or watch, but could not easily reply.\n\n[I] The spread of the internet in the 1990s meant that audiences could not only receive information but also respond to it, publish their own views and share content with people around the world. [II] The launch of social networks, such as Facebook in 2004, and of video platforms, such as YouTube in 2005, made these activities part of everyday life. [III] News no longer travels in only one direction, from a few large organisations to the public. [IV] Today, a teenager with a smartphone can reach more people than a nineteenth-century newspaper could.\n\nEach new medium has raised hopes and fears. Printing was criticised for spreading dangerous ideas, and television was often blamed for keeping children away from books. Today, people worry about false information and screen addiction. History suggests, however, that societies eventually learn to use new media wisely and develop rules and habits to deal with <b>them</b>."
