        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "A documentary is a film or programme that presents real events, people and places rather than fictional stories. Well-known examples include nature series such as Planet Earth, which uses spectacular photography to show animals in their natural habitats. Because documentaries combine pictures, sound and expert commentary, they can explain complicated topics in a way that is easy to <b>grasp</b>.\n\nDocumentaries have long been valued in education. Teachers use them to bring history, science and geography to life, and students often remember what they see on screen more easily than what they read in a textbook. <u>Some documentaries have even changed public attitudes, for example by drawing attention to the damage that plastic waste causes to the oceans.</u> Many viewers say that such films made them change their own habits.\n\nHowever, viewers should remember that a documentary is not a neutral mirror of reality. Filmmakers choose what to show and what to leave out, and the music, editing and commentary can <b>influence</b> how viewers feel. A film may also present only one side of a controversial issue. For this reason, it is wise to compare several sources, especially when a documentary deals with politics or science.\n\nTeachers can help students to become critical viewers. After watching a documentary, teachers might ask: Who made it, and why? What evidence is given? Which voices are missing? Students who practise such questions learn that <b>they</b> do not need to accept everything on screen, and this skill will be useful for the rest of their lives."
