        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "The word 'podcast' was created in 2004 by combining 'iPod' and 'broadcast'. A podcast is a series of audio programmes that listeners can download or stream whenever they like. Unlike traditional radio, it is not tied to a <b>fixed</b> schedule: people can pause an episode, replay it or listen to it at a different time. Today, hundreds of millions of people around the world listen to podcasts on topics ranging from news and history to comedy and language learning.\n\nOne reason for the popularity of podcasts is their intimacy. Many are recorded by one or two presenters who speak in a natural, conversational style, so listeners may feel that they are sharing a chat with a friend. <u>Producing a podcast is also much cheaper than making a television programme, which allows people with little money to reach an audience on very specialised subjects.</u> As a result, there are podcasts for almost every interest, from astronomy to traditional cooking.\n\n[I] Podcasts also have limitations. [II] Because anyone can publish one, the quality and accuracy of the content vary greatly, and some presenters offer opinions as if they were facts. [III] In addition, many shows are paid for by sponsors, whose products are mentioned by the presenters during the programme. [IV] For this reason, regulators in several countries require podcasters to tell their audiences when a message is a paid promotion.\n\nDespite these concerns, podcasts are becoming part of education. Teachers use them to give students extra listening practice, and some universities record lectures as podcasts so that students can revise on the move. Language learners, in particular, benefit from hearing natural speech, and many of <b>them</b> say that listening for a few minutes every day has improved their confidence."
