        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "In a democratic society, journalists play several important roles. They inform citizens about events in their community and in the wider world, so that people can make decisions about their lives. They also act as 'watchdogs', investigating those who hold power and <b>exposing</b> wrongdoing when they find it. Without a free press, serious problems such as corruption or dangerous products might remain hidden for years.\n\nTo do their job well, journalists must follow professional standards. They should check facts with more than one source, give people accused of wrongdoing a chance to reply and clearly separate news from opinion. <u>When a mistake is made, a responsible news organisation corrects it openly, because readers' trust depends on honesty far more than on any claim of perfection.</u> Many outlets publish codes of ethics so that the public can judge whether they are living up to their promises.\n\nJournalism can also be dangerous. Reporters covering conflicts, disasters or organised crime sometimes face threats, and every year some lose their lives while doing their work. Organisations that defend press freedom have called for better protection and for governments to <b>investigate</b> attacks on reporters thoroughly.\n\nJournalism today faces another challenge: financial pressure. As readers move online and advertising income falls, many local newspapers have closed or reduced their staff, leaving some communities with little coverage of local issues. Some outlets are experimenting with paid memberships and donations, hoping that readers who value reliable information will support <b>them</b>."
