        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Have you ever clicked on a headline such as 'You Won't Believe What Happened Next' and then felt disappointed by the article? Headlines like this are known as clickbait. Their purpose is to <b>provoke</b> curiosity or strong emotion so that readers click on the link, because more clicks usually mean more advertising income for the website.\n\nClickbait works because it exploits a natural human instinct. When people notice that a piece of information is missing, they feel a strong desire to fill the gap, a feeling that psychologists sometimes call a 'curiosity gap'. <u>Headlines that promise a surprising secret or a shocking result are therefore difficult to ignore, even for readers who know that they are being manipulated.</u> Using numbers, as in 'Ten Habits of Successful People', has a similar effect, because readers expect a short and easy list.\n\n[I] The disadvantages of clickbait are clear. [II] The headline often promises much more than the article delivers, so readers are left feeling cheated. [III] Moreover, because the aim is to attract attention, the story may be exaggerated or even partly invented. [IV] Over time, readers may lose trust in the websites that rely on this technique.\n\nFortunately, readers can protect themselves. They can look at the source, read beyond the headline and be suspicious of articles that use extreme words or many exclamation marks. Some newsrooms now measure how many readers finish an article, not only how many click on it, and they rewrite headlines that disappoint. Teachers also encourage students to question what <b>they</b> read before sharing it."
