        "passage": "The mass media do not simply report events; they also help to shape what people think about. In the early 1970s, two American researchers, Maxwell McCombs and Donald Shaw, published a study of an election campaign and found that the topics which the news <b>emphasised</b> most were the same topics that people considered most important. As an earlier scholar had put it, the press may not be very successful in telling people what to think, but it is remarkably successful in telling them what to think about.\n\nThis effect is known as agenda-setting. Editors decide which stories appear on the front page or at the start of the news bulletin, and audiences usually take these choices as a sign of importance. <u>A story that is repeated for several days may therefore seem more serious than another that receives a single short report, even when the second one affects more people.</u> In this way, the media can influence public discussion without openly telling anyone what to believe.\n\n[I] Another way in which the media shape opinion is framing. [II] The same event can be presented in different ways, depending on which facts, words and images are chosen. [III] For instance, a report about a new factory may focus on the jobs that it will create, or on the pollution that it might cause. [IV] Neither version has to be false, yet each leaves the audience with a different impression.\n\nDoes this mean that audiences are helpless? Most researchers say no. People bring their own experiences, values and discussions with friends and family to the news, and <b>they</b> often question or reject messages that do not match what they already know. Nevertheless, being aware of agenda-setting and framing helps audiences to read, watch and listen more critically."
