        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Every new form of communication changes the way people use language. Text messages, social media and online chat have produced a vocabulary of abbreviations and symbols that would have puzzled people thirty years ago. Emojis, small pictures that express feelings or ideas, first appeared on Japanese mobile phones in the late 1990s and are now used by people all over the world. In 2015, a 'face with tears of joy' was even chosen as the 'Word of the Year' by the Oxford Dictionaries, a sign of how <b>widespread</b> such symbols had become.\n\nSupporters point out that emojis can make digital messages friendlier. In face-to-face conversation, we rely on tone of voice, facial expressions and gestures, but these are missing from a written text. <u>A smiling face or a thumbs-up can therefore signal that a comment is meant as a joke or as encouragement, and so prevent misunderstandings that plain words might cause.</u> Many companies now use emojis in their advertisements and customer messages to sound more approachable.\n\nCritics, however, worry about the effect on language skills. They fear that constant use of abbreviations such as 'u' for 'you' may weaken students' spelling and make formal writing sloppy. Research so far has produced <b>inconsistent</b> results: some studies find a small negative effect, while others find none, and a few even suggest that frequent texters have a stronger interest in words.\n\nMost linguists therefore take a relaxed view. Language has always changed, they say, and people are usually able to switch between styles, writing casually to friends and formally in an essay. The important skill is knowing which style suits the situation. Teachers can help students to develop this awareness by asking <b>them</b> to rewrite informal messages in a formal tone."
