        "passage": "The mass media are not only a source of news and entertainment; they can also be a powerful tool for improving public life. Governments, charities and health organisations regularly use television, radio, posters and social media to run campaigns that encourage people to change their behaviour. Typical campaigns <b>promote</b> healthy habits, road safety, environmental protection or vaccination.\n\nSome campaigns have been remarkably successful. In Vietnam, for example, a law requiring motorcycle riders to wear helmets came into force in December 2007. The law was supported by a large media campaign that explained the risks of head injuries and showed real stories of accident victims. <u>Within a short time, helmet use rose sharply, and health experts believe that many lives have been saved as a result.</u>\n\n[I] Such results, however, do not happen automatically. [II] The message must be simple and clear, and it should speak directly to the audience's own concerns. [III] Emotional stories and familiar faces can attract attention, but a campaign also has to show people what they can do and make the desired action easy. [IV] Finally, the campaign must be repeated through several channels, because people rarely change their habits after hearing a message only once.\n\nCampaigns can fail, too. Messages that frighten people too much may make them avoid the subject, and messages that sound like orders can cause resentment. Today, social media offers new possibilities: campaigners can target specific groups, such as teenagers, and invite audiences to share their own stories. Experts advise organisers to listen to the people they hope to reach, since <b>they</b> are more likely to respond to messages that reflect their own lives."
