        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Protected areas such as national parks and nature reserves are the cornerstone of wildlife conservation. Within their boundaries, hunting and logging are controlled, and animals can breed with less disturbance. Yet protection on paper does not guarantee protection in practice. Many reserves are short of money and staff, and rangers may have to patrol areas as large as small provinces with only a handful of colleagues. This is why local support is so <b>essential</b>.\n\nCommunities living near reserves often depend on forests for fuel, food and building materials. If they are forbidden to enter without being offered alternatives, they may feel resentful and may ignore the rules. <u>Conservation projects that share benefits with villagers, by offering jobs or a part of the tourism income, tend to win much greater cooperation.</u> Some projects train former hunters to become guides or rangers, turning people who once threatened wildlife into its protectors.\n\n[I] Technology is also changing the work of rangers. [II] Camera traps record the movements of shy animals without disturbing them. [III] Satellite images reveal where forests are being cleared, and mobile apps allow patrol teams to record the locations of snares and illegal camps. [IV] These tools provide evidence that helps authorities to take action more quickly.\n\nStill, no technology can replace public support. When people understand why wildlife matters, <b>they</b> are more likely to report poaching, to refuse wildlife products and to support laws that protect nature. Education programmes in schools and media campaigns therefore form an essential part of conservation, alongside patrols and laws."
