        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "The pangolin, a small mammal covered in hard scales, is often described as the most heavily trafficked wild mammal in the world. Four species live in Asia and four in Africa, and all of them are protected by international law. Yet demand for their meat and scales continues, and their numbers have fallen <b>sharply</b> in many countries.\n\nPangolins are shy animals that roll into a ball when they feel threatened. This defence works against predators, but it makes them easy for poachers to pick up. They also breed <b>slowly</b>, usually producing only one baby at a time, so populations cannot recover quickly when too many animals are taken. <u>Unless the demand for their products falls, even strict laws may not prevent their disappearance.</u>\n\nConservation groups are trying to help in several ways. Rescue centres care for pangolins that have been seized from traffickers, and, when the animals are healthy, they release them in protected forests. Customs officers are being trained to recognise pangolin scales, and some countries have raised penalties for people involved in the trade. In addition, scientists are studying how the animals live in the wild, because little is known about their habits.\n\nPublic education is just as important as law enforcement. Many consumers do not know that pangolins are endangered, and some believe that their scales have medical value, although tests have found no evidence of this. Campaigns that explain these facts can reduce demand. Pangolins in protected forests are more likely to survive than <b>those</b> living near villages and roads, but only if people stop buying their products."
