        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Sea turtles have lived in the world's oceans for over a hundred million years, yet most of the seven species are now threatened with extinction. Females spend most of their lives at sea, but they return to lay eggs on the same kind of sandy beach where they were born, sometimes after more than twenty years. In Vietnam, Con Dao National Park is one of the most important nesting areas for green turtles, and every year rangers and volunteers <b>patrol</b> its beaches to protect the nests.\n\nThe dangers they face begin before they hatch. Eggs are collected by people for food, and beaches are lost to hotels and roads. Artificial lights confuse the hatchlings, which normally crawl toward the brightest horizon, the moonlit sea, and lead them inland instead. <u>Even those that reach the water face a long struggle: it is estimated that perhaps only one in a thousand survives to adulthood.</u> At sea, turtles are caught accidentally in fishing nets, and some swallow plastic bags that they mistake for jellyfish.\n\n[I] Fortunately, many conservation measures are succeeding. [II] On several beaches, nests are marked and guarded, or the eggs are moved carefully to protected hatcheries until they are ready to hatch. [III] Hotels and local authorities in some regions also switch off or shield their lights during the nesting season, and nets fitted with escape devices allow turtles to get out if they are caught. [IV] Education matters, too: tourists are now told not to touch nests or use flash photography near nesting turtles.\n\nIndividuals can help, too. Visitors to nesting beaches should keep their distance, avoid using lights and never take eggs or shells as souvenirs. At home, people can reduce plastic waste by using cloth bags and refusing single-use straws, because much of the plastic in the sea comes from the land. Scientists agree that the survival of sea turtles will depend on cooperation between governments, businesses and ordinary people, so <b>they</b> all have a part to play."
