        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Some people find wild animals fascinating and want to keep them at home. Monkeys, parrots, snakes and exotic reptiles are all sold as pets in various countries. However, wild animals are not like cats or dogs, which have been bred for thousands of years to live with humans. Even when raised from a very young age, a wild animal keeps its natural instincts and may become <b>aggressive</b> or destructive as it grows.\n\nBehind the pet trade lies a cruel process. Many animals are taken from the wild, often after their parents have been killed, and are transported in small containers without proper food or water. A large number die before they reach a buyer. <u>Because the trade is profitable, hunters continue to capture more animals, and some species have become rare in the wild as a result.</u> Animals that survive may be kept in conditions that are far from their natural environment, with the wrong food, temperature or space.\n\nThere are risks for people, too. Wild animals can carry diseases that can spread to humans, and some bite or scratch when frightened. Owners who realise that they cannot cope often release their pets into the countryside. This is harmful: the animals may not survive, or, if they do, they can <b>threaten</b> local wildlife. In Florida, for example, Burmese pythons that were released or escaped have become established in the wild and are blamed for a sharp decline in some small mammals.\n\nPeople who love animals have better options. They can adopt a cat or a dog from a shelter, volunteer at a licensed rescue centre or visit protected areas, where wildlife can be admired without being disturbed. Governments are also tightening laws, but laws work best when buyers cooperate. If fewer people are willing to pay for wild animals, traders will have less reason to capture <b>them</b>."
