        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Each year, billions of birds undertake journeys that seem almost impossible. The bar-tailed godwit, a wading bird, has been recorded flying more than 11,000 kilometres from Alaska to New Zealand without stopping, in little over a week. Other birds follow routes called flyways, which pass through many countries and depend on a chain of places where the travellers can rest and <b>refuel</b>.\n\nWetlands, such as marshes, mudflats and mangrove forests, are among the most important of these stopping places. Their shallow waters contain an abundance of shellfish, worms and small fish, which are exactly the food that exhausted birds need. <u>For some species, a single wetland may provide the only opportunity along thousands of kilometres to regain strength before the journey continues.</u> In Vietnam, Xuan Thuy National Park in the Red River Delta, recognised as a Ramsar site in 1989, welcomes thousands of waterbirds every winter.\n\n[I] The reason is that wetlands are disappearing: scientists estimate that the world has lost about a third of them since 1970. [II] Many have been drained for farming, filled in for construction or polluted by waste. [III] Climate change adds to the pressure, because rising seas flood coastal habitats and droughts dry up inland ones. [IV] As a result, birds find fewer places to rest, and populations of many migratory species are falling.\n\nFortunately, there are signs of hope. International agreements such as the Ramsar Convention, signed in 1971, encourage countries to protect their most valuable wetlands, and many have created reserves and restored damaged areas. Local communities also play a part: in some places, fishermen have learned to collect shellfish in ways that do not harm the habitat, and <b>they</b> now earn a living from guiding bird watchers."
