        "passage": "Elephants are the largest land animals on Earth, and in Asia they have been part of human life for thousands of years. Asian elephants have been used to carry heavy loads, to work in forests and to take part in ceremonies. In Vietnam, wild elephants once <b>roamed</b> many forested regions, but their numbers have fallen sharply. Today, only a small number are thought to remain, mostly in a few provinces such as Dak Lak, Dong Nai and Nghe An.\n\nElephants face several threats. The most serious is the loss of habitat: forests have been cleared for farms, roads and dams, so herds have less space to feed and move. <u>When elephants leave the remaining forests, they may eat or trample crops, and angry villagers sometimes harm them in return.</u> Poaching is another danger, since some people hunt elephants for their ivory or meat, even though the practice is illegal. Because elephants breed slowly, a reduced population can take decades to recover.\n\n[I] Captive elephants face problems of their own, because for many years tourists in parts of Southeast Asia have paid to ride them or to watch them perform tricks. [II] Despite this popularity, welfare experts point out that elephants used in this way are often trained by harsh methods and may suffer from heavy loads and a lack of rest. [III] In response, some tourism operators have begun to offer elephant-friendly experiences, in which visitors simply observe the animals from a distance. [IV] In Dak Lak, one well-known tourist site announced that it would stop elephant rides in 2018 and encourage walking tours instead.\n\nEven so, tourism alone cannot save wild elephants. Conservationists stress that the animals need enough forest, safe routes between habitats and the support of nearby communities. Some projects help farmers to protect their fields with fences or watch towers, so that villagers see elephants as less of a threat. Education also matters: when children learn about elephants at school, <b>they</b> often carry the message home to their families."
