        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Primates, the group of animals that includes monkeys, apes and humans, are among our closest living relatives. Vietnam is home to a remarkable variety of them, including several kinds of langur that live nowhere else or almost nowhere else in the world. These <b>distinctive</b> animals, known for their striking colours and long tails, have become symbols of the country's rich biodiversity.\n\nSadly, many of these primates are in danger. Habitat loss is a major cause: forests have been cleared for farms and plantations, leaving small patches where groups are isolated from one another. <u>Hunting makes matters worse, because primates are killed for food, for use in traditional medicine or to be sold as pets, even though such trade is illegal.</u> Because they reproduce slowly, a small population can take many years to grow again, even when the threats are removed.\n\n[I] Rescue centres in Vietnam provide a refuge for primates seized from illegal traders or handed in by owners who could no longer keep them. [II] There the animals are checked by vets, treated for illness and fed a healthy diet. [III] Those that are healthy enough may later be prepared for a return to the wild, although this is difficult and takes a long time. [IV] Others, which cannot be released because of injury or the lack of survival skills, stay in the centre and help to teach visitors about their species.\n\nRescue centres alone cannot save the primates, however. Conservationists stress that the best way to protect them is to protect their forest homes and to reduce the demand for wildlife products. Local people play a key role: when villagers are given other sources of income and learn about the value of the animals, <b>they</b> are less likely to hunt them. Education programmes in schools can also help young people to see primates as a national treasure rather than a source of money."
