        "passage": "The Mekong, which flows through six countries before reaching the sea in southern Vietnam, is one of the richest rivers in the world for fish. Scientists believe that it is home to more than a thousand fish species, a number matched by very few other rivers. Among them is the Mekong giant catfish, which can grow to about three metres in length and is regarded as one of the largest freshwater fish. For millions of people who live along the river, fish are not only a main source of protein but also a <b>vital</b> part of local livelihoods.\n\nMany Mekong fish are migratory: they travel long distances between feeding areas and the places where they breed. <u>Dams built across the river can block these routes, so fish cannot reach their breeding grounds, and populations downstream may fall.</u> Dams also trap sand and nutrients that would otherwise be carried to the delta, which affects farming and the coastline. Other pressures include overfishing, the use of very fine nets and sand mining, which damages riverbeds where fish lay their eggs.\n\n[I] Protecting a river as large as the Mekong requires cooperation across borders. [II] Governments in the region share information through the Mekong River Commission, an organisation that monitors water levels and fish catches. [III] At the local level, some villages have set up community fish refuges, where fishing is banned in deep pools that act as shelters during the dry season. [IV] Researchers also work with dam operators to test fish passages that may allow fish to swim around or over obstacles, although the results so far have been mixed.\n\nThe future of the river fish is therefore uncertain. Climate change adds to the problem, since droughts and rising seas may alter the flow of water and increase the amount of salt in the delta. Experts stress that planners should consider the effects of new dams on local fishers, because <b>they</b> are often the first to notice changes in the river. If governments, communities and scientists continue to work together, there is still a chance to protect this extraordinary river and the animals that depend on it."
