        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Whales are intelligent mammals that depend on sound. Underwater, light travels only a short distance, so whales use sounds to find food, to navigate and to <b>communicate</b> with one another across great distances. Some species, such as the humpback whale, produce complex songs that can last for many minutes.\n\nThe oceans have become much noisier over the past century. <u>The engines of ships, the seismic surveys used to search for oil and building work in coastal waters all produce loud sounds that can travel for many kilometres underwater.</u> Scientists believe that this noise can mask the calls of whales, making it harder for them to find partners or food, and in some cases it may cause them to change their routes.\n\nShips are a danger in other ways. Whales that swim near busy shipping lanes may be struck by vessels, and large ships find it hard to <b>avoid</b> an animal once it has been seen. Whales can also become entangled in fishing nets and lines, from which they may be unable to escape.\n\nSolutions exist. Ships can be asked to slow down in areas where whales are known to gather, which reduces both noise and the risk of collisions. Quieter engines and propellers are being developed, and some countries have moved shipping lanes away from important feeding grounds. Experts say that such measures will work only if the companies that operate ships agree to follow the rules, because <b>they</b> are the ones who decide how fast and where vessels travel."
