        "passage": "Extinction is the permanent disappearance of a species. Although it is a natural process, scientists believe that today the rate of loss is far higher than the natural rate, mainly because of human activities such as habitat destruction, hunting and pollution. To measure the danger, the International Union for Conservation of Nature, or IUCN, keeps the Red List of Threatened Species, which ranks species according to how close they are to disappearing and has been <b>compiled</b> since the 1960s.\n\nSpecies on the Red List are placed in categories. \"Vulnerable\" species are likely to become endangered unless the threats are reduced. \"Endangered\" species face a very high risk, and \"critically endangered\" species face an extremely high risk of disappearing in the wild. <u>The assessments are based on factors such as population size, the rate at which numbers are falling and the area in which a species lives.</u> Experts review the information regularly, so a species can move to a more or a less serious category as conditions change.\n\n[I] Sadly, some species have already been lost. [II] In 2010, the last known individual was found dead in Cat Tien National Park, and examinations indicated that it had been shot by poachers. [III] Elsewhere, the Yangtze river dolphin, once common in China, has not been confirmed in the wild since the early 2000s and is widely regarded as functionally extinct. [IV] Such losses show how quickly a small population can vanish once the threats become too great.\n\nExtinction is not always inevitable. The IUCN reports that some species have moved to a lower category of risk after conservation programmes, which shows that action can work. Protecting habitats, enforcing laws against hunting and educating the public all make a difference. Because many species disappear before scientists have even described them, researchers stress that whole ecosystems deserve protection, since <b>they</b> shelter thousands of forms of life at once."
