        "passage": "Bees are among the most important pollinators in the world. When a bee visits a flower to collect nectar, grains of pollen stick to its body and are carried to the next flower, <b>enabling</b> the plant to produce seeds and fruit. Scientists estimate that a large share of the world's food crops, including many fruits, nuts and vegetables, depend at least partly on pollination by insects.\n\nPollinators also support wild ecosystems. <u>Many wild plants rely on insects to reproduce, and these plants in turn supply food and shelter for birds, mammals and other animals.</u> If pollinators were to disappear, the effects would spread through whole food chains.\n\nHowever, pollinators are in trouble in many regions. The causes include the loss of flowering meadows, the use of some chemicals on crops and diseases carried by parasites. Climate change may also <b>disrupt</b> the timing between the flowering of plants and the appearance of insects.\n\nIndividuals can help. Planting a variety of flowers that bloom at different times of the year, leaving a corner of the garden uncut and avoiding unnecessary chemicals all give bees food and shelter. Farmers can sow strips of wildflowers beside their fields, and cities can plant flowers along roads and on rooftops. Some scientists say that wild bees thrive when <b>they</b> can find food all year round."
