        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Stories about extinction can make conservation seem hopeless, but there are also real success stories. They show that, with strong laws, scientific knowledge and public support, even species on the edge of disappearing can <b>recover</b>. Three well-known examples come from very different parts of the world: the giant panda, the humpback whale and the bald eagle.\n\nIn China, the giant panda was once threatened by the loss of its bamboo forests. The government created nature reserves, banned hunting and planted new forests that link isolated habitats. <u>In 2016, the IUCN moved the species from \"endangered\" to \"vulnerable\" after surveys suggested that its wild population had grown to roughly 1,800 animals.</u> Experts warn, however, that climate change may reduce the area of bamboo in the future, so the panda still needs protection.\n\n[I] Humpback whales were hunted almost to the point of disappearing in the twentieth century because their oil and meat were valuable. [II] After an international agreement ended most commercial whaling in the 1980s, many populations began to grow, and the species is now classified as \"least concern\". [III] In North America, the bald eagle suffered badly from the widespread use of the pesticide DDT, which made the shells of its eggs thin and weak. [IV] After DDT was banned in 1972 and laws protected the birds, they recovered, and in 2007 they were removed from the American list of endangered species.\n\nThese successes did not happen by accident. In each case, scientists first identified the cause of the decline, and governments then passed laws that people were willing to follow. Local communities also played a part: villagers near some panda reserves, for example, have been trained as rangers, and <b>they</b> now help to monitor the forests. Nevertheless, conservationists remind us that recovery can take decades, and that animals which have been saved still need long-term protection."
