        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Wildlife is not found only in forests and oceans. As cities grow, many animals have learned to live alongside people, and some even seem to <b>thrive</b> there. Foxes, pigeons, bats and many kinds of insects can be found in busy urban areas, where food waste, gardens and warm buildings offer plenty of resources.\n\nCities can even benefit certain species. <u>Parks, ponds and green roofs create small habitats where birds, bees and butterflies can find food and shelter, and some of these species are becoming rare in the countryside.</u> City dwellers also gain from wildlife: studies suggest that spending time in green spaces can reduce stress and improve mood.\n\nYet living in cities also brings dangers for animals. Traffic kills large numbers of animals each year, and glass windows are <b>deadly</b> for birds that cannot see them. Artificial light can confuse nocturnal animals and migrating birds, while rubbish may poison or trap those that eat it.\n\nResidents can make cities friendlier to wildlife. Planting native flowers, putting up nest boxes and leaving a little water in a shallow dish are simple steps. Local governments can protect green corridors, such as river banks and tree-lined streets, so that animals can move safely between parks. Experts also advise residents not to feed wild animals, since <b>they</b> may lose their fear of humans and become a nuisance."
