        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Roads and railways are essential for modern life, but they cut through the habitats of wild animals. A road may look like a narrow line on a map, yet for many animals it is a barrier that is difficult or <b>hazardous</b> to cross. Traffic kills large numbers of animals every year, and the noise and lights of busy roads may keep others away from areas where they would normally feed or breed.\n\nA second problem is fragmentation. <u>When a road divides a forest into two parts, animals living on either side may be unable to meet, which means that each group becomes smaller and more isolated.</u> Small, isolated populations are at greater risk, because they have fewer mates to choose from and are more likely to suffer if disease, fire or bad weather strikes. Over time, the lack of new genetic material can make such populations weaker.\n\n[I] The best-known of these is the wildlife crossing, which can be a bridge built over a road or a tunnel built under it. [II] In Banff National Park in Canada, dozens of such structures have been built, and studies there suggest that collisions between vehicles and large animals have fallen sharply. [III] Smaller measures also help, such as fences that guide animals towards crossings and pipes under roads for amphibians. [IV] Planners may also protect or restore green corridors, which are strips of natural habitat that connect separate areas of forest.\n\nSuch projects are expensive and cannot be built everywhere. However, planners increasingly agree that it is more efficient to include wildlife measures when a road is first designed than to add them later. Animals also need time to learn to use crossings; some species begin to use them quickly, while others may take years. Ecologists therefore monitor the crossings with cameras to see which animals use <b>them</b>, and the results help to improve future designs."
