        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Fish have been an important source of food for thousands of years, and today they provide protein for billions of people. However, many fish populations are now in trouble. Overfishing occurs when fish are caught faster than they can <b>reproduce</b>, so that the number of fish in the sea keeps falling. According to some estimates, a significant proportion of the world fish populations are fished at unsustainable levels.\n\nOverfishing harms more than the species that are targeted. <u>Large fishing nets often catch other animals by accident, such as turtles, dolphins and sea birds, and these animals are usually thrown back into the sea dead or injured.</u> Some methods, such as dragging heavy nets along the seabed, can also destroy habitats like coral reefs. When top predators such as tuna or sharks are removed, the balance of the whole ecosystem can be disturbed.\n\nMany people depend on fishing for their livelihood, so solutions must be fair. Governments can set limits on the amount of fish that may be caught, close certain areas during the breeding season and require nets that let young fish escape. Some countries also <b>restrict</b> the use of destructive methods. Such rules work only if they are enforced, which is difficult in large areas of open sea.\n\nConsumers also have a role. Many shops and restaurants now offer seafood that has been certified as sustainable, and shoppers who choose it send a message to the industry. Eating a wider variety of fish, rather than only a few popular kinds, can reduce pressure on the species that are most at risk. Fishing communities themselves often support these efforts when <b>they</b> understand that healthy fish stocks are essential to their future income."
