        "passage": "Vietnam is one of the most biologically diverse countries in the world. Its long coastline, mountains, rivers and tropical forests provide a home to thousands of species of plants and animals, many of which are found nowhere else. In recent decades, scientists have also made some <b>remarkable</b> discoveries in the Annamite Mountains along the border with Laos, where several new species of large mammals have been identified.\n\nThe best-known of these finds is the saola, a rare forest-dwelling relative of cattle with two long, straight horns. <u>When it was first recorded in 1992 in the Vu Quang Nature Reserve, it was the first large mammal new to science in more than fifty years, and it quickly became a symbol of the hidden wildlife of Vietnam.</u> Nicknamed the \"Asian unicorn\", it is so shy that very few scientists have ever seen one in the wild.\n\n[I] The saola is now critically endangered, and the main danger comes from snares, which are set in the forest to catch other animals such as deer and wild pigs but also trap saola by accident. [II] Because the saola lives in remote and steep forests, patrols to find and remove these snares are demanding and costly. [III] These teams, including rangers and local villagers, have removed large numbers of snares in recent years. [IV] Some experts are also considering a breeding programme to save the species, although finding enough animals to start one would be difficult.\n\nThe story of the saola carries a wider message. Vietnam may still hold species that science has not yet described, but each year more land is cleared for farms and roads. Protecting large, connected areas of forest is therefore one of the best ways to safeguard species that are still unknown. Local communities near the forest also deserve support, because <b>they</b> are the ones who can protect wildlife in the long run."
