        "passage": "Conservationists once relied mainly on binoculars, notebooks and long hours in the field. Today, they use a wide range of technology to <b>monitor</b> wildlife more accurately and over larger areas. Camera traps, for example, are cameras with motion sensors that photograph animals without disturbing them, and they can reveal the presence of species that are almost never seen by people.\n\nSatellites and drones provide a different view. <u>Images taken from above can show how fast forests are shrinking, and drones can count animals in areas that are too large or too dangerous for people to cover on foot.</u> Some drones are also used to detect illegal activities, such as logging or fishing in protected areas.\n\nGenetic techniques have opened another door. Scientists can now identify a species from a tiny sample of hair, skin or even water, because every animal leaves traces of its DNA behind. This method is especially useful for animals that are shy or <b>elusive</b>, since researchers can detect them without ever seeing them. DNA tests can also reveal whether products sold in markets come from protected species.\n\nTechnology cannot replace people, however. Cameras and drones produce huge amounts of data, which experts must still analyse before any decisions can be made. New tools can also be expensive, and countries with the richest biodiversity often have the smallest budgets. For this reason, some organisations train local rangers to use the equipment, so that when the machines give a warning, <b>they</b> can react quickly."
