        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Many students feel overwhelmed by the number of careers to choose from, so they turn to tests that promise to reveal the ideal job. One of the best-known career theories was developed by the American psychologist John Holland, who suggested that people and work environments can both be grouped into six types: realistic, investigative, artistic, social, enterprising and conventional. According to this theory, people tend to be happier when their work environment <b>matches</b> their personality.\n\nOnline tests based on such ideas ask users about their preferences and then suggest a list of suitable occupations. For example, a person who enjoys helping others and working in groups may be advised to consider teaching or nursing. <u>Although these tests can provide a useful starting point, psychologists point out that the results depend on how honestly and carefully the questions are answered.</u> Moreover, a test measures only some aspects of a person, and it cannot predict how well someone will perform in a job.\n\n[I] Career tests can be helpful, but they should be used with care. [II] For instance, talking to people who actually do the jobs that interest you can give a far more realistic picture of daily work. [III] Work experience, such as a short internship or a volunteer project, also allows young people to test their interests in practice. [IV] Finally, school counsellors can help students to bring all this information together and to set realistic goals.\n\nCareer choices are also not final. Many people change direction at least once, and the skills that workers gain in one job can often be used in another. Experts therefore advise young people to treat a test result as a suggestion, not a verdict. Teachers and parents can help by encouraging teenagers to try different activities, since <b>they</b> often discover unexpected talents in this way."
