        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Healthcare is one of the largest and fastest-growing sectors in the world. When people think of working in healthcare, they often picture doctors and nurses, but the sector also includes pharmacists, laboratory technicians, physiotherapists, radiographers, dietitians and many other professionals. Each of these roles is <b>crucial</b> because modern medicine depends on teamwork.\n\nTraining for these jobs is demanding. Doctors usually study for six years or more at university and then spend further years in hospitals before they can specialise. Nurses and technicians typically complete shorter programmes, but they, too, need practical training in real hospital wards. <u>Since mistakes in healthcare can affect the lives of patients, students must learn to work accurately and to stay calm under pressure.</u> Because medical knowledge changes quickly, professionals must also continue to study throughout their careers.\n\nThe rewards of healthcare careers are considerable. Many professionals say that they feel proud when they help patients to recover, and there is usually strong demand for qualified staff. Nevertheless, the work can be physically and emotionally <b>exhausting</b>: shifts may be long, and staff often deal with suffering and loss. Hospitals therefore try to provide support, such as counselling and flexible schedules, to protect the well-being of their employees.\n\nTechnology is also changing healthcare. Telemedicine allows doctors to examine patients through video calls, and artificial intelligence can help to read medical images. These tools will not replace healthcare workers, however. Patients still need compassion and human contact, and <b>they</b> are likely to go on valuing personal care even as machines become more capable."
