        "passage": "STEM stands for science, technology, engineering and mathematics. Careers in these fields are in high demand because new technologies, from renewable energy to artificial intelligence, depend on skilled people. In many countries, STEM graduates are also often well paid, and their skills are <b>versatile</b>, so they can be applied in a wide range of industries.\n\nDespite these opportunities, women remain under-represented in many STEM fields. In engineering and computer science, for example, men hold a large majority of jobs in many countries. <u>Researchers suggest that stereotypes, a lack of female role models and a belief that these subjects are \"not for girls\" discourage many young women from even considering such careers.</u> Girls often perform as well as boys in science and mathematics at school, yet fewer of them choose to study these subjects at university.\n\n[I] Many schools and organisations are trying to change this situation. [II] Some run summer camps in which girls build robots or write simple programs. [III] Others invite women scientists and engineers to talk about their work and to act as mentors. [IV] Teachers can also help by encouraging girls to take part in science clubs and competitions.\n\nCompanies can contribute as well. Some offer internships and flexible working hours to attract and keep female engineers, and a number of them sponsor scholarships for girls who study technical subjects. Experts argue that a more balanced workforce benefits everyone, because teams with different backgrounds often solve problems more creatively. Girls themselves should remember that talent has no gender, and that <b>they</b> can succeed in any field if they have interest, effort and support."
