        "passage": "Creative industries, such as design, animation, music, film and video games, are growing in many countries. Careers in these fields require imagination and artistic skills, but they also demand technical knowledge. A video game artist, for instance, must be able to draw characters and also to use complex software to <b>animate</b> them.\n\nMany young people are discouraged from choosing creative careers because they are seen as unstable. <u>It is true that artists, musicians and designers often work on short-term projects and that their income may rise and fall from month to month.</u> However, creative skills are increasingly valued outside traditional art fields: companies need designers for websites and apps, writers for marketing and animators for training videos. People with creative talent can therefore find jobs in almost every industry.\n\nSuccess in a creative career depends on more than talent. A strong portfolio, a collection of the best examples of a person's work, is often more important than a certificate, because employers want to see what candidates can actually produce. Creative workers also need to build networks, <b>accept</b> criticism and keep learning new tools, since software and styles change quickly.\n\nEducation can prepare young people for creative careers, but it cannot guarantee success. Many creative professionals are self-taught, learning from online courses, tutorials and practice. Parents and teachers can support talented teenagers by encouraging them to build skills in both art and technology, and by showing that <b>they</b> take creative careers seriously."
