        "passage": "Tourism is one of the world's biggest employers, and it has become an important part of the economy in many countries, including Vietnam. In 2019, about eighteen million foreign visitors came to Vietnam, and tourism has since <b>rebounded</b> after the pandemic years. Behind every holiday are many people at work: tour guides, hotel receptionists, chefs, travel agents, pilots and drivers.\n\nCareers in tourism and hospitality are attractive to many young people because they offer variety and contact with people from different cultures. Entry-level jobs often do not require a university degree, so young workers can start early and learn on the job. <u>Foreign language skills are especially valuable, since employees who can communicate with visitors in several languages are more likely to be promoted.</u> Good manners, patience and a friendly attitude are equally important, because customers remember how they were treated.\n\n[I] However, working in tourism has its difficulties. [II] Demand is often seasonal, so staff may be very busy during holidays and have little work in quiet months. [III] For example, shifts frequently include evenings, weekends and public holidays, when other people are free. [IV] Salaries at the beginning of a career may also be modest, although they can rise with experience and responsibility.\n\nDespite these drawbacks, many workers find the industry rewarding. Travel and hospitality training is available at colleges and universities, and some hotels run their own programmes to train young staff. Sustainable tourism is also creating new jobs, such as guides who explain local nature and culture to visitors. People who enter the industry should be flexible and curious, because <b>they</b> may be asked to take on new roles as travel patterns change."
