        "passage": "A mentor is an experienced person who offers guidance to someone who is less experienced. In the workplace, a mentor might be a senior colleague who shares knowledge about the company, gives feedback and helps a newcomer to <b>navigate</b> unfamiliar situations. Mentors are also found in schools, sports clubs and community groups.\n\nThe benefits of mentoring go in both directions. <u>Mentees gain advice, confidence and contacts, while mentors often find that explaining their ideas to others helps them to see their own work more clearly.</u> Many organisations also report that mentoring programmes can encourage staff to stay longer, because employees feel supported.\n\nGood mentoring relationships do not happen by accident. The mentee should be willing to listen, ask questions and act on advice, and the mentor should be patient and <b>honest</b>. Both people need to agree on clear goals, such as improving a particular skill or preparing for a promotion. Experts suggest that regular meetings, even short ones, are more effective than occasional long talks, because progress is easier to follow.\n\nYoung people who cannot find a mentor at work or school can look elsewhere. Online platforms connect learners with volunteers in many professions, and professional associations often organise mentoring schemes for students. Some people also benefit from several mentors at the same time, since <b>they</b> can offer different perspectives on a single problem."
