        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Teaching is one of the oldest professions, and in many cultures teachers are treated with great respect. In Vietnam, for example, Teachers' Day on 20 November is celebrated with flowers, cards and school ceremonies. Yet being a good teacher is not simply a matter of knowing a subject. It requires patience, creativity and the ability to <b>motivate</b> students of different abilities and backgrounds.\n\nTeachers do much more than stand in front of a class. They plan lessons, mark assignments, meet parents and sometimes organise clubs and trips. <u>Because the workload extends beyond official hours, many teachers say that the job is demanding, even though they find it rewarding.</u> In several countries, schools also struggle to attract enough teachers for subjects such as mathematics, science and foreign languages, partly because graduates in these fields can earn more in other industries.\n\nBecoming a teacher usually involves several stages of preparation. [I] First, future teachers study their subject together with teaching methods and child psychology at a university or training college. [II] They then spend time in real classrooms, where they teach under the supervision of an experienced teacher. [III] After qualifying, new teachers often receive guidance from colleagues during their first years. [IV] Throughout their careers, they are expected to update their knowledge, for example by attending workshops and learning to use new technologies.\n\nThe profession is also changing. Digital tools allow teachers to share materials and give feedback quickly, and many schools now encourage them to design projects instead of simply delivering lectures. At the same time, society expects teachers to support the mental health of students and to prepare them for jobs that do not yet exist. Teachers who stay curious and flexible usually find the work more satisfying, because <b>they</b> can adapt to new situations and enjoy learning alongside their students."
