        "passage": "Green jobs are jobs that help to protect or restore the environment. They exist in many sectors, including renewable energy, waste management, sustainable agriculture, forestry and green building. Some, such as solar panel installer or wind turbine technician, are completely new, while others, such as farmers or engineers, are traditional jobs that have been <b>adapted</b> to more sustainable methods.\n\nDemand for such workers is growing as governments set targets to reduce carbon emissions. <u>International organisations have estimated that the shift towards a low-carbon economy could create millions of new jobs worldwide, although some jobs in fossil fuel industries are likely to disappear.</u> Governments and companies are therefore investing in retraining programmes so that workers can move into new sectors.\n\nGreen jobs do not always require advanced degrees. Technicians and installers often learn through vocational courses or apprenticeships, whereas designers, scientists and policy specialists usually need university qualifications. Whatever the level, employers look for workers who understand sustainability and who are willing to learn new technologies. A positive attitude towards change is <b>essential</b>, because the sector is developing rapidly.\n\nYoung people who care about the planet can prepare by studying relevant subjects such as science, geography or engineering, and by joining environmental clubs or volunteering for conservation projects. Employers say that candidates with this experience stand out, because <b>they</b> have shown real commitment as well as knowledge."
