        "passage": "Agriculture is often seen as an old-fashioned sector, yet it remains vital to Vietnam. The country is one of the world's leading exporters of rice, coffee, pepper and cashew nuts, and a substantial share of the workforce still depends on farming and related industries. Feeding a growing population while protecting the environment, however, is a <b>formidable</b> challenge that requires skilled professionals as well as hard-working farmers.\n\nA wide range of careers exists in this field. Agronomists advise farmers on soils, seeds and pests; food technologists develop methods to store and process products safely; veterinarians protect the health of livestock; and agricultural engineers design machines and irrigation systems. <u>Many of these professionals now work with digital tools, such as drones, satellite images and sensors, which help them to decide when to water, fertilise or harvest.</u> Others work in business, helping farmers to sell their products at fair prices, either directly to consumers or through online platforms.\n\n[I] Climate change has become one of the biggest concerns for farmers. [II] In particular, droughts, floods and rising sea levels in the Mekong Delta threaten the production of rice and shrimp. [III] Scientists are therefore developing crop varieties that tolerate salt or heat and are teaching farmers more efficient ways to use water. [IV] Young professionals who can combine biology, technology and business are likely to find that their skills are in demand.\n\nAgricultural careers are also becoming more attractive to young people who return to their home provinces after studying in cities. Some set up their own farms or cooperatives, using social media to market their products directly to customers. Others join large companies that export to foreign markets. Whichever path they choose, graduates need to continue learning, because <b>they</b> work in a field where technology and market demands are changing quickly."
