        "passage": "Money is part of every organisation, from a small shop to a multinational company, so finance and accounting offer a wide range of careers. Accountants record and check financial transactions, prepare reports and help businesses to follow tax laws. Financial analysts study markets and companies in order to <b>advise</b> investors, while bank officers help individuals and firms to save, borrow and invest their money.\n\nAccounting careers usually require a university degree in accounting, finance or a related subject. Many professionals also take additional examinations to earn international qualifications, which can improve their prospects. <u>Because rules and technology change quickly, financial professionals must keep updating their knowledge, and many employers pay for training to help them do so.</u> Attention to detail is essential, since a small mistake in a calculation can have serious consequences.\n\n[I] Technology is transforming the field. [II] Software can now process routine tasks, such as entering invoices or checking figures, in a fraction of the time that a person would need. [III] Some people therefore worry that accountants will be replaced by machines. [IV] Experts expect the role to shift towards analysis and advice, since computers cannot judge unusual situations or explain the meaning of numbers to a client.\n\nDespite these changes, demand for financial skills is likely to remain strong. Start-ups need advice on managing cash, families need help with investments and retirement, and governments need to monitor public spending. Young people interested in this field can begin by learning to manage their own budgets and by joining school economics clubs, where <b>they</b> can practise analysing real data."
