        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Architecture and construction shape the world in which we live. Architects design buildings, from family homes to hospitals and airports, taking into account safety, cost and appearance. Their drawings are then turned into reality by civil engineers, surveyors, electricians, carpenters, welders and many other skilled workers who <b>collaborate</b> on every project.\n\nBecoming an architect requires a long period of study, typically five years or more at university, followed by practical experience before a person can work independently. <u>Besides drawing skills, architects need to understand mathematics and materials, and they must be able to explain their ideas clearly to clients who are not specialists.</u> Today, most of the design work is done with computer software, which allows architects to create three-dimensional models and to test how a building will use energy.\n\nConstruction work, by contrast, is often physical and takes place outdoors. Workers on a building site must follow strict safety rules, wear protective equipment and cope with heat, rain and noise. Nevertheless, the trades offer solid career prospects: skilled workers are needed in almost every country, and experienced employees can progress to become supervisors or start their own firms. Many of them take pride in the visible results of their <b>labour</b>, which may stand for decades.\n\nThe industry is also changing. Environmental concerns have created demand for sustainable buildings that use less energy, recycled materials and renewable power. Builders increasingly use prefabricated parts, which are made in factories and assembled on site. This approach saves time and reduces waste, so companies that adopt it can often offer lower prices because <b>they</b> complete projects faster."
