        "passage": "Not every career is motivated mainly by money. Social workers, charity staff, counsellors and community organisers devote their working lives to helping others. Social workers, for instance, support children, families and elderly people who face difficulties such as poverty, illness or violence, and they help them to find <b>resources</b> such as housing, healthcare and education.\n\nCharities and non-governmental organisations offer a variety of roles, from fundraising and event planning to research and communications. Many have small teams, so employees often perform several tasks at once and gain broad experience quickly. <u>Although salaries in the non-profit sector are often lower than in business, many workers say that they accept this because they feel that their work has a clear purpose.</u> Volunteering is a common first step, and some organisations later hire volunteers who have proved their commitment.\n\n[I] Careers of this kind can be emotionally demanding. [II] Staff often meet people in great distress, and they must stay calm and compassionate even when problems seem impossible to solve. [III] For this reason, good employers provide regular supervision, training and time for rest. [IV] Anyone considering this field should be honest with themselves about how they cope with stress and whether they can set healthy limits.\n\nDespite the challenges, many people find such careers deeply satisfying. Studies in several countries suggest that workers who see a direct benefit to others often report high levels of job satisfaction. Universities offer degrees in social work and community development, and some employers accept graduates in related fields such as psychology or education. Young people who are interested should volunteer first, because <b>they</b> will learn whether this type of work suits their personality."
