        "passage": "Aviation is a glamorous industry, but behind every flight is a large team of professionals. Pilots and cabin crew are the most visible, yet airlines and airports also rely on air traffic controllers, engineers, baggage handlers, ground staff and safety inspectors. Each of them must follow strict procedures, because even a small error can have <b>serious</b> consequences.\n\nBecoming a pilot requires a combination of education, training and health. Candidates must pass medical examinations, complete flight training that can take a year or two, and record a minimum number of flying hours. <u>Since the training is expensive, many students apply for scholarships or loans, and some airlines run cadet programmes that pay part of the costs in exchange for a work contract.</u> After qualification, pilots usually begin as co-pilots and may be promoted to captain after several years of experience.\n\nCabin crew and ground staff follow different paths. Entry requirements often include good communication skills, a pleasant manner and a high level of English, and training courses usually last a few weeks or months. The work can be <b>tiring</b>: crew may spend long hours on their feet, cope with changing time zones and deal with passengers who are anxious or upset. Even so, many employees enjoy the chance to travel and to meet people from different countries.\n\nThe industry is changing as technology develops. Maintenance engineers use sensors that warn them of faults before a failure occurs, and airports are introducing automatic check-in and security systems. Despite this, human skills remain essential, especially in emergencies, when passengers rely on the crew to stay calm and when <b>they</b> expect clear instructions."
