        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "A career is rarely a straight line. Most people begin in an entry-level position, where they learn basic tasks and gain a general understanding of the organisation. With experience, they may take on more responsibility and move up a career ladder, for example from assistant to supervisor and then to manager. Progress, however, depends on more than the <b>length</b> of service.\n\nEmployers consider several factors when deciding whom to promote. Results are important, but so are teamwork, reliability and a willingness to learn. <u>Employees who volunteer for difficult projects and who help colleagues usually attract attention, because they show that they can take initiative and think beyond their own tasks.</u> Regular feedback from managers also helps workers to understand which skills they need to improve.\n\nNot everyone, however, wants to climb the ladder in this way. [I] Some, for example, prefer to become specialists, developing deep expertise in a single field instead of managing other people. [II] Others move sideways, changing departments or industries to gain a broader range of skills. [III] Such lateral moves can be valuable, because they make a worker more adaptable if the company reorganises. [IV] Whichever route a person chooses, it is wise to discuss goals regularly with a manager or mentor.\n\nWhatever path is chosen, the labour market is changing quickly, and the skills that employers value today may not be the same in ten years. Workers who keep learning new skills, for example through short courses, are more likely to stay employable. Companies also benefit when employees feel that <b>they</b> have opportunities to grow, since staff who see a future in an organisation tend to stay longer."
