        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "When choosing a career, young people often feel pulled in two directions. Some advisers encourage them to follow their passion, arguing that people who love their work are more motivated and more likely to succeed. Others warn that a passion may not provide a stable income and recommend choosing a profession with good <b>prospects</b>.\n\nBoth views contain some truth. <u>Interest can sustain a person through the difficult early years of a career, but enthusiasm alone does not pay the bills if few employers need the skills involved.</u> At the same time, a well-paid job that a person finds boring may lead to dissatisfaction, and some workers eventually leave such positions to look for greater meaning.\n\nFortunately, the choice is not always so stark. Many people discover that their interests overlap with areas of strong demand. A person who loves drawing, for instance, might combine art with technology in animation or user-interface design; someone who enjoys nature might work in environmental consultancy. Researching the job market can help to <b>identify</b> such overlaps, and short projects or internships allow young people to test them.\n\nIt is also worth remembering that neither interests nor job markets stay fixed. Passions may grow or fade, and industries that are booming today may decline tomorrow. For this reason, advisers suggest that young people develop transferable skills, such as communication and problem-solving, so that <b>they</b> can adapt when circumstances change."
