        "passage": "Technology has always changed the world of work. The steam engine, the telephone and the computer each made some jobs disappear and created others. Today, many experts believe that artificial intelligence, or AI, will bring another <b>profound</b> change, although they disagree about how quickly this will happen.\n\nAI is already used in many workplaces, and some tasks are changing faster than others. <u>Routine tasks that follow clear rules, such as entering data or checking simple documents, are the easiest for machines to take over, whereas jobs that need creativity, empathy or complex decisions are harder to automate.</u> Teachers, nurses and counsellors, for example, spend much of their time understanding the feelings of other people, which machines cannot yet do well.\n\n[I] Experts warn that two problems need attention if societies are to benefit from AI. [II] The second concerns workers, who may have to learn new skills after some of their tasks are replaced, which takes time and money. [III] Schools and companies can help by teaching digital skills and encouraging people to keep learning throughout their lives. [IV] Those who adapt quickly are likely to find more opportunities than those who do not.\n\nIn the end, the future of work will depend on the choices that people make today. Young people choosing careers should therefore avoid looking only at what is popular now. Instead, they should ask which skills are likely to stay useful, such as communication, critical thinking and creativity. Employers also say that <b>they</b> value people who can learn quickly and work well in teams."
