        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Networking means building and keeping relationships with people who may help you in your career, yet it is often <b>misunderstood</b>. Many people imagine that it is only for confident adults who attend business events. In fact, it can begin much earlier, because school clubs, volunteer projects and online communities all give students a chance to meet people with similar interests.\n\n<u>A good network is not built by collecting as many contacts as possible, but by showing real interest in other people and offering help whenever you can.</u> Someone who shares useful information, congratulates a colleague on a success or introduces two people who may benefit from knowing each other is remembered in a positive way.\n\nNetworking can also go wrong. People who contact others only when they need a favour, such as a recommendation or a job, may be seen as <b>selfish</b>. Experts therefore advise keeping in touch regularly, even with a short message, and saying thank you after receiving help.\n\nReputation matters as well. Today, many employers search for the names of candidates online before inviting <b>them</b> to an interview, so public posts and photos may influence their opinion. Young people are therefore encouraged to think carefully before posting, and to use professional websites to present their skills and achievements. A positive online image does not replace real relationships, but it can support them."
