        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Few people today can expect to work in the same job, using the same skills, for forty years. Machines are taking over routine tasks, new industries are appearing, and employers keep changing the abilities they require. As a result, workers are urged to <b>upgrade</b> their skills regularly, either to improve in their current roles or to prepare for new ones.\n\nThis process is known as upskilling when it deepens existing abilities, and reskilling when it prepares someone for a different occupation. A bank clerk who learns data analysis is upskilling; a factory worker who trains as a technician is reskilling. <u>Both approaches help people to remain employable when the demand for certain jobs falls.</u> Employers benefit too, since it is usually cheaper to train existing staff than to recruit new ones.\n\n[I] Opportunities for learning have never been more plentiful. [II] Online platforms offer thousands of courses, many of them free, from programming to public speaking. [III] Local libraries, community centres and trade unions organise workshops for adults. [IV] Some companies pay for employees to attend classes or give them time during working hours to study.\n\nYet the biggest obstacle may be attitude rather than access. Many adults believe that they are too old to learn or fear that they will look foolish among younger students. Research on the brain, however, shows that people can learn at any age, although they may need more time to master some skills. Learners who approach new topics with curiosity usually progress faster than <b>those</b> who expect to fail."
