        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Learning a foreign language is often considered a task for the young, but older adults can also succeed. Many retirees take up Spanish, French or English and enjoy the challenge of mastering new vocabulary. Although it is true that children pick up accents more easily, adults have advantages of their own.\n\nAdults can use strategies that children do not. They can compare grammar with that of their first language, plan their studies and apply what they learn immediately in real situations. Learners who set clear goals and practise regularly usually improve faster than <b>those</b> who study only when they have time. <u>Studies suggest that using a language actively, by speaking and writing rather than merely listening, is the most effective way of remembering it.</u>\n\nLearning a language also benefits the brain. It requires attention, memory and problem-solving, and some researchers believe that it may help to delay the signs of age-related decline. Bilingual people are often better at switching between tasks, and they tend to be more <b>flexible</b> thinkers. Whether or not these effects are large, the social benefits are clear: speaking another language opens doors to new friendships, travel and cultures.\n\nOf course, progress is not always smooth. Adults may feel embarrassed when they make mistakes, and busy schedules can interrupt study. The best remedy is patience. Setting small, regular targets, such as learning ten new words a day, and celebrating each success keeps motivation high. Over time, the effort <b>accumulates</b>, and learners often surprise themselves with how much they can do."
