        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Many students believe that the best way to prepare for an exam is to read the same pages again and again. Research on learning, however, suggests that this habit is not very <b>efficient</b>. Rereading creates a feeling of familiarity, which can be mistaken for real understanding, and it often leaves people surprised when they cannot remember the facts later.\n\nMany psychologists therefore recommend two techniques. The first is retrieval practice, in which learners close the book and try to write down or say what they remember. <u>Although this method feels harder than rereading, it forces the brain to search for information, and this effort tends to strengthen memory.</u> The second technique is spacing, which means dividing study time into several short sessions instead of one long one.\n\n[I] Sleep, for example, plays an important part. [II] Researchers believe that the brain organises new information while people sleep, so students who study late into the night may remember less than those who rest. [III] Short breaks during study can also help, since concentration tends to fall after long periods without rest. [IV] A short walk or a glass of water may be enough to refresh the mind.\n\nNone of these methods is a magic solution. Learners differ, and what works well for one person may work less well for another. Teachers can help by explaining the techniques and encouraging students to test <b>them</b> in practice. Over time, students are likely to discover a combination of methods that suits their own subjects and habits."
