        "passage": "Learning is often imagined as a <b>solitary</b> activity, but psychologists who study motivation have drawn attention to the role of beliefs. A growth mindset is the belief that abilities can be developed through effort, good strategies and help from others. By contrast, a fixed mindset is the belief that talent is something people are born with and cannot change.\n\n<u>Research suggests that learners with a growth mindset tend to persist longer when tasks become difficult, because they see difficulty as a normal part of learning rather than as proof of low ability.</u> They are also more likely to ask for feedback and to try different strategies. However, the evidence is still debated, and some studies have found only small effects.\n\nTeachers and parents can encourage this mindset in simple ways. Praising effort and strategy, rather than intelligence alone, may help children to <b>persevere</b>. Saying \"You have not mastered it yet\" instead of \"You are bad at this\" reminds learners that progress is possible. Adults can also set an example by talking openly about their own mistakes and by showing that they are still learning.\n\nA growth mindset is not a cure for every problem, however. Effort alone will not help if a learner uses poor methods or lacks support, and no one should be blamed for circumstances beyond their control. Schools should therefore give students good strategies and resources, so that <b>they</b> can turn effort into progress."
