        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Educators often distinguish three types of learning: formal, non-formal and informal. Formal learning takes place in schools, colleges and universities, follows a fixed curriculum and usually leads to a recognised qualification. For many people, this is the type of learning that comes to mind first, and it forms the <b>foundation</b> for further study and employment.\n\nNon-formal learning is organised, but it takes place outside the official education system. Examples include language classes at community centres, workshops run by youth organisations and training courses offered by employers. <u>Because these programmes are usually more flexible than school, they can respond quickly to the changing needs of learners, although they do not always lead to a certificate.</u>\n\n[I] Informal learning is the least organised type, and people often do not even notice that it is happening. [II] They pick up knowledge from conversations with family and friends, from television and the internet, and from simply trying things out at home or at work. [III] A child who learns to cook by watching a parent, for example, is learning informally. [IV] For this reason, informal learning is seldom recorded on a certificate, even though it can be very powerful.\n\nIn practice, the three types of learning overlap. A student may study mathematics at school, join a coding club at the weekend and then learn from online videos at home. Many experts therefore argue that education systems should recognise the skills that people gain outside school, so that <b>they</b> can use them to find work or continue their studies."
