        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Many people picture learning as a quiet, individual task, yet it can be much more effective when people learn together. A study group is a small number of learners who meet regularly to discuss what they are studying, solve problems and prepare for tests. Such groups can be organised at school, in a library or online, and they have become <b>increasingly</b> common among university students.\n\n<u>When learners explain an idea to others, they are forced to organise their thoughts, and this process often shows them which parts they have not understood properly.</u> Hearing different explanations can also help, since a classmate may describe a difficult concept in a way that is easier to follow than the textbook. In addition, a group can give its members encouragement on days when motivation is low.\n\nStudy groups do not always work well, however. Some members may rely on others to do all the work, while some may be easily <b>distracted</b> and turn the meeting into a chat. To avoid these problems, groups should agree on a clear goal for each session, give every member a task and stay small, perhaps between three and six people.\n\nOnline technology has widened the possibilities. Learners can now join discussion forums, video meetings or messaging groups with people from different cities or even different countries. This allows <b>them</b> to hear viewpoints that they might never meet in their own classroom. Nevertheless, a few face-to-face meetings are still valuable, because personal contact tends to build trust more quickly."
