        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Today, anyone with a phone can find an answer to almost any question within seconds. Yet the internet also contains a great deal of false or misleading information, and it can be difficult to tell the difference. For this reason, educators regard critical thinking, the ability to question and evaluate what we read, hear and see, as an essential skill for lifelong learners. Without it, people may accept claims that are <b>dubious</b> simply because they appear on a professional-looking website.\n\nResearchers who study how people judge websites have found that even confident students are sometimes misled. <u>Fact-checkers behave differently from many readers: instead of staying on a single page, they open new tabs to see what other sources say about the website that they are reading.</u> This simple habit can quickly reveal who is behind a site and whether other sources trust it.\n\n[I] Educators also suggest asking a few simple questions about any source. [II] The first is who produced the information and what their purpose might be. [III] The second is what evidence supports the claim, and whether it comes from a reliable study or only from personal opinion. [IV] The third is when the information was published, because facts that were correct ten years ago may be out of date today.\n\nCritical thinking is not the same as distrusting everything. The aim is to become a careful reader who is willing to change an opinion when good evidence appears. Teachers can help young people to develop this skill by discussing news stories in class and asking <b>them</b> to explain how they decided what to believe. With regular practice, checking sources can become as natural as checking the time."
