        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "In many countries, young people who do not wish to go to university can learn a trade through vocational education and training. Programmes range from a few months to several years and cover fields such as construction, hospitality, healthcare and information technology. Because they emphasise practical skills, vocational courses are often more directly linked to the needs of employers than general academic programmes. Some learners combine study with paid work, which allows them to earn money while they <b>acquire</b> a qualification.\n\nApprenticeships are one well-known form of vocational training. <u>An apprentice is employed by a company and spends part of the week learning on the job from experienced workers and the rest in a college, so theory and practice reinforce each other.</u> Countries such as Germany and Switzerland are often cited as places where this system has long been valued, although apprenticeships also exist in many other countries.\n\n[I] In spite of these advantages, vocational education has an image problem in some societies. [II] Many parents and students regard a university degree as the only route to a respectable career, and they see practical courses as a second choice. [III] For this reason, several governments have begun campaigns to improve the image of vocational careers. [IV] Some schools also invite graduates of vocational courses to talk to students about their jobs.\n\nLooking ahead, vocational education may become even more important. Technology is changing the skills that workplaces require, and learners of any age may need short, practical courses to update what they know. Colleges that cooperate closely with employers can adjust their programmes quickly and offer <b>them</b> to adults as well as to school leavers."
