        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "As more services move online, from banking to medical appointments, older adults who do not use digital technology may find daily life more difficult. This gap is sometimes called the digital divide. Contrary to common belief, many seniors are not unwilling to learn; they are often held back by a lack of confidence, by fear of making costly mistakes or by <b>unfamiliar</b> vocabulary such as \"cloud\" and \"app\".\n\n<u>Courses designed specifically for older learners tend to be successful when they proceed slowly, allow plenty of practice and let participants work on tasks that matter to them, such as video-calling relatives.</u> Many community centres and libraries now offer such classes, and some are taught by teenage volunteers who enjoy sharing their knowledge.\n\nLearning technology can bring real benefits. Older adults who use video calls can keep in touch with family members who live far away, which may reduce feelings of <b>isolation</b>. Online services also make it easier to shop, to manage money and to find information about health. In addition, learning a new skill gives a sense of achievement that may improve confidence in other areas of life.\n\nSafety must not be forgotten, however. Older people are sometimes targeted by criminals who send false messages asking for money or personal details. Teachers should therefore show learners how to recognise suspicious messages and should remind <b>them</b> never to share passwords or bank codes with anyone. With patience and respect, digital skills can be taught at any age."
