        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "The idea that education should continue throughout life is not new, but it gained international attention in the second half of the twentieth century. Organisations such as UNESCO argued that rapid social and economic change made it impossible for schools to teach everything that people would need during their lives. In a famous report published in 1996, a commission led by Jacques Delors described four <b>pillars</b> of education: learning to know, learning to do, learning to live together and learning to be.\n\nThese four pillars show that lifelong learning is not only about jobs. <u>Learning to live together, for example, refers to understanding other people and working with them peacefully, while learning to be concerns the full development of a person's mind, body and personal values.</u> Supporters argue that this broad vision helps societies to prepare citizens who are skilled, responsible and open to different cultures.\n\n[I] In Vietnam, too, the idea of a learning society has been promoted in education policy in recent years. [II] Many communes and wards have community learning centres, where local people can attend classes in practical skills, health care and digital technology. [III] These centres are often run with limited budgets, yet they give residents who left school early a second chance to learn. [IV] Their success depends on teachers, local organisations and volunteers who are willing to work together.\n\nCritics point out that policies alone are not enough. Learning opportunities must be affordable and easy to reach, particularly for people in rural areas, and employers must allow workers time to study. When schools and workplaces value learning, <b>they</b> create an environment in which people of any age are encouraged to develop."
