        "passage": "Many adults say that they would love to play a musical instrument but believe it is too late to begin. Although children may have some advantages, such as more free time, adults also have strengths: they understand why they are practising, they can organise their own study and they are usually more <b>committed</b> to the instrument they have chosen.\n\n<u>Research on the brain suggests that learning to play music involves many skills at the same time, such as listening, coordinating the hands and reading symbols, and some studies indicate that these activities may be good exercise for the brain at any age.</u> However, scientists are still investigating how large the benefits are and for whom.\n\nBeginners can make progress more smoothly if they follow a few simple <b>principles</b>. Short, regular practice sessions, even ten minutes a day, are usually more effective than a single long session once a week. Starting with easy pieces builds confidence, and recording oneself can help learners to notice mistakes that they would otherwise miss.\n\nPlaying with others can be especially rewarding. Many towns have amateur orchestras, choirs and bands that welcome beginners, and group lessons give learners the chance to meet people with similar interests. Even when progress seems slow, playing together tends to keep <b>them</b> motivated, since the music is enjoyable and the support of friends makes practice less lonely."
