        "passage": "Employers around the world often say that technical knowledge is not enough. In surveys and reports, they repeatedly mention soft skills such as communication, teamwork, problem-solving and adaptability. Unlike technical skills, which relate to a specific job, soft skills are useful in almost every workplace and are therefore often described as <b>transferable</b>.\n\nSoft skills are not learned in the same way as formulas or dates. <u>Because they develop through practice and reflection rather than memorisation, activities such as group projects, debates and volunteering can be more useful than lectures for building them.</u> A student who leads a team, for example, gradually learns how to listen, to share tasks and to deal with disagreement.\n\n[I] Schools have begun to pay more attention to these abilities. [II] Some teachers use project-based learning, in which students work in groups to solve real problems over several weeks. [III] Others invite local professionals to speak about the skills that they use every day. [IV] This is not easy, however, because teamwork and communication are harder to measure in a test than facts.\n\nSoft skills also matter beyond the workplace. People who can listen carefully and manage conflict tend to have better relationships, and they are often more able to learn from others. Since the world of work keeps changing, these abilities are worth developing throughout life, because <b>they</b> stay useful through many different jobs."
