        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Volunteering is usually seen as a way of helping others, but it can also be a powerful form of learning. People who give their time to a charity, a hospital or a community project often acquire skills that are rarely taught in classrooms, such as dealing with unexpected problems, working with people of different ages and managing their time. For young people, volunteering offers a chance to try out activities before committing to a career, while for adults it can provide <b>fresh</b> challenges after years in the same job.\n\n<u>Studies suggest that volunteers often report greater confidence and a stronger sense of purpose, although it is difficult to prove that volunteering itself is the cause, since people who are already confident may be more likely to volunteer.</u> This caution is important because it reminds us that two things that appear together are not always connected as cause and effect.\n\nTo get the most from volunteering, learners should treat it as an experience to reflect on. Writing a short diary, discussing difficulties with a supervisor and asking for feedback can turn a pleasant activity into a lasting lesson. It is also wise to choose a role that matches one's interests, because a volunteer who finds the work <b>meaningful</b> is more likely to continue.\n\nOrganisations benefit as well. Many charities depend on volunteers, and they must therefore provide training, clear instructions and recognition for the work. Volunteers who feel respected are more likely to stay, and <b>they</b> often bring friends and family members to help."
