        "passage": "In many countries, retirement is no longer seen as the end of learning. Older people often have more free time, and many want to use it to pursue interests that they could not follow earlier in life. Education providers have responded by creating courses designed for this group, ranging from history and art to computing and languages. One well-known example is the University of the Third Age, often shortened to U3A, which began in France in the 1970s and later <b>spread</b> to many other countries.\n\nA distinctive feature of many U3A groups is that the members themselves act as both teachers and students. <u>Instead of paying professional teachers, a group of retired people with similar interests may meet regularly, and each member shares whatever knowledge he or she has with the others.</u> This model keeps costs low and recognises that older people bring a lifetime of experience that others can learn from.\n\n[I] Research suggests that continuing to learn in later life may bring several benefits. [II] People who join courses often report that they feel more connected to others and more satisfied with life. [III] Some studies also indicate that mentally active people may be less likely to experience a rapid decline in memory, although the evidence is not yet conclusive. [IV] Whatever the exact effects, there is little doubt that learning gives many older people a stronger sense of purpose.\n\nOf course, older learners face obstacles. Some live far from classes, some have health problems, and others lack confidence in using technology. Many providers now offer online lessons and home visits so that <b>they</b> can take part even when travelling is difficult."
