        "passage": "Travel is often described as the best teacher, and there is some truth in this idea. Visiting an unfamiliar place forces people to deal with new languages, customs and situations, and these challenges can build independence and open-mindedness. Even short trips to nearby towns can be educational, provided that travellers are willing to <b>immerse</b> themselves in local life by talking to residents, trying traditional food and visiting ordinary places rather than only famous attractions.\n\n<u>Travel is not automatically educational, however; a person who stays in a hotel, eats only familiar food and spends the day taking photographs may return home with few new ideas.</u> The difference lies in the attitude of the traveller. Those who prepare by reading about the history of a place, learn a few phrases of the local language and reflect on what they see are likely to learn more.\n\nIndependent and low-budget travel can also teach practical skills. Planning a route, comparing prices and solving problems such as a missed train require creativity and calm thinking, and these experiences may increase a traveller's <b>resilience</b>. Many young people also say that meeting people from different backgrounds changes the way they see the world.\n\nTechnology has changed the way people travel and learn. Translation apps, online maps and video guides make it easier to understand unfamiliar places, but they cannot replace real conversations. Travellers who put their phones away from time to time often notice details that <b>they</b> would otherwise miss, such as the sounds of a market or the gestures of a stranger."
