        "passage": "For many years, scientists believed that the adult brain was fixed: after childhood, they thought, it could not change much, and any damage was permanent. Research over the past few decades has shown that this picture was too simple. The brain keeps its ability to form new connections between nerve cells throughout life, a property known as neuroplasticity. Although this ability is generally greater in children, it does not <b>vanish</b> in adulthood.\n\nA well-known example comes from studies of people who spend years learning complex skills. <u>Brain scans of musicians and of people who have memorised the streets of a large city show differences in certain brain regions, which suggests that intensive practice can shape the brain in measurable ways.</u> Such findings do not prove that anyone can become an expert, but they support the idea that effort leaves lasting traces.\n\n[I] Scientists have identified several factors that seem to support learning in adulthood. [II] Regular physical exercise appears to help the brain by improving blood flow. [III] Sleep is another important factor, as it allows the brain to store what has been learned. [IV] In addition, trying new and challenging activities, rather than repeating familiar ones, seems to encourage the brain to adapt.\n\nThese findings should encourage learners rather than make them anxious. They do not mean that learning will always be easy, and some skills do become harder to acquire with age. However, they suggest that learners who keep practising can continue to improve, so <b>they</b> should not accept the belief that it is too late to start."
