        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Digital technology is changing how people learn. Online platforms can record what a learner has already mastered and suggest activities for the next stage, a process often called personalised learning. In theory, a student who finds fractions difficult can receive extra practice on fractions, while a classmate who has already mastered them moves ahead. Supporters believe that this approach saves time and keeps learners <b>engaged</b>, because the tasks are neither too easy nor too hard.\n\nArtificial intelligence has recently added new possibilities. AI tutors and chatbots can answer questions at any hour, explain a concept in several ways and give immediate feedback on practice exercises. <u>Their availability is especially valuable for adult learners, who often study late in the evening when no human teacher is available.</u>\n\n[I] However, experts also warn about several risks. [II] AI systems can produce answers that sound confident but are wrong, so learners need to check important information against reliable sources. [III] Some learners may also rely on these tools to do the thinking for them, which could weaken their own skills. [IV] For these reasons, teachers advise using technology as a support for learning rather than as a replacement for effort.\n\nLooking ahead, the most successful learners are likely to be those who combine technology with human contact. Teachers, mentors and fellow students offer encouragement and different viewpoints that no program can fully provide. Online communities also let learners share their difficulties and celebrate progress, which keeps <b>them</b> motivated during long courses."
